UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' Under the sliding filament model, a sarcomere shortens because the thin (actin) filaments are pulled to slide past the thick (myosin) filaments toward the center, drawing the Z-discs closer together; the filaments themselves do not shorten. The A band corresponds to the full length of the thick filaments, whereas the I band is the region occupied only by thin filaments not yet overlapped by thick filaments. (Choice B) is correct: because thick filament length is fixed, the A band stays the same width, while the thin filaments sliding inward reduce the non-overlap zone, so the I band narrows. As the sarcomere contracts, the Z lines move closer together and the I band becomes smaller, while the A band retains its width. (Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the relationship. Students who assume the darkest, thickest-looking band must be the part that "contracts" wrongly shrink the A band and leave the I band fixed, but the A band tracks the unchanging thick-filament length. (Choice C) is incorrect: it assumes the whole sarcomere shrinks uniformly like a stretched spring relaxing, but shortening comes from filament sliding, not from any band's own filaments compressing, so the A band width is preserved. (Choice D) is incorrect: if neither band changed, the sarcomere could not shorten at all; the I band must narrow as the zone of overlap increases. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the sliding filament model to predict which band width is constrained by fixed thick-filament length and which changes as overlap increases.$px$ WHERE id = '697f24c3-3998-4d76-bab5-6a9e42e037fb';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is B because the band 3 anion exchanger is an electroneutral antiporter: each negatively charged bicarbonate ion driven out of the cell (down the gradient created by ongoing CO2 hydration) obligatorily brings one negatively charged chloride ion in, so no net charge crosses the membrane and electroneutrality is maintained. (Choice B) is correct: the exchanger swaps one anion out for one anion in, so chloride enters specifically to replace the negative charge lost as bicarbonate exits. That is the essence of the chloride shift. (Choice A) is incorrect: the anion exchanger is passive and does not hydrolyze ATP; bicarbonate leaves down its own outward concentration gradient, and chloride entry is coupled to that exchange, not powered by a pump. (Choice C) is incorrect: chloride moves inward, not outward. It never leaves the cell to balance bicarbonate, and reversing the direction of chloride movement contradicts the observed shift. (Choice D) is incorrect: although H+ is generated alongside bicarbonate, it is buffered by hemoglobin inside the cell and does not combine with chloride to form HCl; charge balance is achieved through anion exchange, not acid formation. The skill assessed is reasoning about how coupled membrane transport preserves electroneutrality during CO2 handling in the blood.$px$ WHERE id = '69c659f3-da9e-4f72-bb55-07d7604975b1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Neuron & Glial Cell Structure.' Myelinating glia insulate axons in a lipid-rich sheath, but the two types differ in architecture: a Schwann cell (PNS) surrounds only a single segment of a single axon, whereas an oligodendrocyte (CNS) sends out a few branching processes that each myelinate a separate axon segment, so one cell can service segments on several different neurons. The reconstruction here is in spinal cord white matter (central nervous system) and shows one cell body wrapping five segments across three neurons, so the number of segments served and the location together point to only one cell type. (Choice A) is incorrect: the Schwann cell is a genuine myelinating glial cell, but it operates in the peripheral nervous system, not the spinal cord, and each Schwann cell wraps a portion of only one axon segment, so it could never account for one cell insulating five segments on three neurons. (Choice B) is correct: the oligodendrocyte is the CNS myelinating glial cell whose several processes each reach out to a different axon segment, allowing a single cell to insulate multiple segments across separate axons, exactly the branching one-to-many pattern reconstructed here in central nervous tissue. (Choice C) is incorrect: the astrocyte is a real CNS glial cell, but it supports neurons by regulating extracellular ions, clearing signaling molecules, and forming the blood-brain barrier; it does not produce myelin, so it would not appear as insulating wraps on axon segments. (Choice D) is incorrect: the microglial cell is a real CNS glial cell, but it acts as a resident macrophage that scavenges and degrades dead cells and pathogens; it performs immune surveillance rather than myelination and would not form lipid sheaths on axons. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the one-cell-to-many-segments architecture of CNS myelinating glia, together with the central-nervous-system location, to infer which glial cell type produced the reconstructed pattern.$px$ WHERE id = '69d55470-b690-4b59-a278-de4f21f8c3d3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is B because only statement III correctly pairs a denaturing agent with the interaction it disrupts. Denaturation is the loss of three-dimensional (tertiary and quaternary) structure and function without breaking the primary sequence; each agent disrupts a specific class of stabilizing interaction. Urea and guanidinium chloride unfold proteins through generalized solvent effects: guanidinium chloride, like other salts, denatures proteins without causing them to precipitate, while urea acts through nonspecific solvent interactions with the protein, weakening the noncovalent hydrophobic effect that buries nonpolar side chains in the protein core. (Choice A) is incorrect: beta-mercaptoethanol is a disulfide-reducing agent that cleaves the covalent \(-\text{S}-\text{S}-\) bond of cystine back to two free thiols; it does not hydrolyze backbone peptide bonds, and denaturation leaves the primary sequence intact. (Choice B) is correct: guanidinium chloride and urea unfold proteins by disrupting the noncovalent interactions, including the hydrophobic effect that sequesters nonpolar residues away from water. (Choice C) is incorrect: it pairs the true statement III with the false statement I, which wrongly assigns peptide-bond hydrolysis to a reducing agent. (Choice D) is incorrect: statement II reverses the chemistry. Heat disrupts the weak noncovalent interactions (hydrogen bonds and hydrophobic packing), whereas the covalent disulfide bond is far more resistant and is the target of reducing agents, not heat. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between covalent and noncovalent stabilizing interactions to predict which bonds each denaturing treatment breaks.$px$ WHERE id = '69ed0097-c76d-453f-9e76-95fd3c980634';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is B because the two enzymes attack the same amylose chain in fundamentally different geometric patterns. α-amylase is an endo-acting enzyme: it hydrolyzes α-1,4 bonds at random internal positions along the chain. β-amylase is an exo-acting enzyme: it removes one maltose unit at a time only from the non-reducing end. When each enzyme has cleaved the same small number of α-1,4 bonds, the location of those cuts is what matters. Each internal cut by α-amylase splits a long polymer into two shorter pieces, so a few random internal cuts sharply lowers the average chain length while liberating very little free maltose (most fragments are still oligomers longer than a disaccharide). In contrast, each β-amylase cut merely shaves a maltose off the end, so the same number of cuts produces a stoichiometric amount of free maltose while the bulk polymer barely shrinks. Comparing aliquot 1 (α-amylase) to aliquot 2 (β-amylase): aliquot 1 has the larger chain-length drop and the smaller maltose pool.

(Choice B) is correct: random internal (endo) cleavage collapses average chain length quickly with little disaccharide release, whereas end-wise (exo) removal of maltose does the opposite, so aliquot 1 loses more chain length and accumulates less maltose than aliquot 2.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the two enzymes. Rapid maltose accumulation with little change in chain length is the signature of the exo-acting β-amylase (aliquot 2), not the endo-acting α-amylase (aliquot 1).

(Choice C) is incorrect: it assumes cleaving the same number of bonds produces the same physical outcome regardless of where the bonds are. Endo versus exo positioning is precisely what makes the outcomes differ, so identical results cannot be expected.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it misassigns products and mechanism. β-amylase must release sugar (maltose) with each cut, so an enzyme that loses chain length "without releasing any sugar" is impossible; and α-amylase, cutting internally, does not preserve the original chain length.

At the Skill 2 level, this requires predicting how enzyme geometry (endo random-internal versus exo end-wise) maps onto two measurable consequences (average chain length and disaccharide yield) rather than recalling a stated definition, and then correctly assigning each predicted pattern to the right enzyme.$px$ WHERE id = '6b45ab7e-bdbc-4af0-92ff-d63a8886631b';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A 52-year-old patient presents with persistent hyperglycemia. Lab work drawn in the fasting state shows a fasting plasma glucose well above normal, yet the patient's serum insulin concentration is simultaneously elevated, markedly higher than that of a healthy fasting individual. Which finding best accounts for this combination?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is A because the coexistence of high glucose AND high insulin can only be reconciled if the beta cells are actively secreting insulin but the target tissues fail to respond to it. Insulin's principal targets, skeletal muscle and adipose cells, normally clear glucose from the blood when insulin binds its tyrosine-kinase receptor and mobilizes glucose-transporter vesicles to the membrane; when these cells are resistant, glucose stays elevated, the pancreas compensates by secreting even more insulin, and both values rise together. This is the defining lesion of type 2 diabetes: reduced sensitivity of tissue cells to insulin rather than a failure to produce it. (Choice A) is correct: target-cell (receptor-level) resistance leaves circulating insulin high while glucose uptake fails, exactly matching the paired high-insulin/high-glucose data. (Choice B) is incorrect: autoimmune loss of beta cells is type 1 diabetes, in which the beta cells produce no insulin. That predicts a LOW serum insulin, directly contradicting the elevated insulin measured here. (Choice C) is incorrect: excess glucagon from alpha cells would raise blood glucose, but glucagon does not cause a compensatory rise in insulin secretion sufficient to explain a markedly elevated fasting insulin, and it is not the primary defect underlying this classic presentation. (Choice D) is incorrect: cortisol excess (as in Cushing syndrome) can raise blood glucose, but the adrenal cortex is not the pancreatic source of insulin, and cortisol-driven hyperglycemia does not itself explain why measured insulin is high. To answer, a student must integrate two data points (high insulin excludes a production failure, while high glucose signals unresolved insulin action) and infer a receptor-level defect rather than reading a definition, the reasoning characteristic of Skill 2.$px$ WHERE id = '6b9be32c-025b-4fad-9565-6e6bcd8f993d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is A because relative (Darwinian) fitness is defined by an individual's reproductive contribution to the next generation measured against the rest of the population, not by its own survival in isolation. Natural selection favors individuals with greater contributions to the gene pool of the next generation, and what matters is not an individual's absolute fitness but how its contribution compares to the other organisms in the population. This comparative measure is relative fitness, which identifies which individuals are adding more offspring to the next generation. Relative fitness thus refers to an individual's ability to survive and reproduce relative to the rest of the population. (Choice A) is correct: surviving offspring measured against others in the population is exactly the comparative reproductive contribution that defines relative fitness. (Choice B) is wrong because fitness is a measure of reproduction, not longevity; an individual can be long-lived yet contribute few offspring, and fitness is framed around contributing additional offspring, not lifespan. (Choice C) is wrong because an absolute offspring count is the individual's absolute fitness, which is explicitly not what counts. Relative fitness compares the individual to the rest of the population. (Choice D) is wrong because chance fluctuation in allele frequencies describes genetic drift, the effect of chance on a population's gene pool, a random force distinct from fitness-based selection. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definition of relative (Darwinian) fitness as a comparative measure of reproductive contribution.$px$ WHERE id = '6bf22818-db7c-43b4-bb5d-d68e170bebf7';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Biosignaling.'

The answer is A because the total output per bound hormone is the product of the step stoichiometries: \(1 \times 70 \times 1 \times 500 = 35{,}000\) molecules. The amplifying steps are the ones with a 1-to-many ratio (Step 2, \(1\to70\), and Step 4, \(1\to500\)); a single activated protein at each of these steps begets many products, so a change there multiplies through everything downstream. Mutation W cuts Step 2 from 70 to 35, giving \(1 \times 35 \times 1 \times 500 = 17{,}500\), a halving of the entire cascade output. Of the four options, only W reduces a genuinely amplifying step in a way that lowers the total, so it produces the largest reduction.

(Choice A) is correct: because Step 2 is a catalytic, one-to-many step, halving its fan-out (70 → 35) propagates multiplicatively and halves the final total from 35,000 to 17,500 molecules per hormone.

(Choice B) is incorrect: Mutation Z changes only the rate of Step 3, not its stoichiometry. The total number of signaling molecules per hormone is fixed by the per-step product counts, not by how fast each step runs; a 1-to-1 step still passes on exactly one activated enzyme per G protein whether it does so quickly or slowly, so the total stays \(1 \times 70 \times 1 \times 500 = 35{,}000\). This reasoning wrongly assumes that speeding (or slowing) a step linearly rescales the whole product, which the stated fixed stoichiometry does not support.

(Choice C) is incorrect: Mutation X doubles Step 3 (1 → 2), which would raise output to \(1 \times 70 \times 2 \times 500 = 70{,}000\), an increase, not a reduction. Choosing X reflects the misconception that the step producing the largest raw count (Step 4's 500) or any downstream change automatically dominates; direction and whether the change increases or decreases fan-out matter.

(Choice D) is incorrect: Mutation Y doubles receptor binding (Step 1 becomes 1 → 2), which increases total output to \(2 \times 70 \times 1 \times 500 = 70{,}000\). Step 1 is a 1-to-1 (non-amplifying) step, and calling it the "only true point of amplification" reverses the actual relationship. The amplifying steps are the catalytic 1-to-many ones (Steps 2 and 4), not the binding step.

At the Skill 2 level, this problem requires you to apply the amplification principle to a novel numbered cascade: recognize that total intracellular response equals the product of per-step fan-out factors, identify which steps are catalytic (one-to-many) versus one-to-one, and predict the quantitative consequence of altering a single step rather than recalling any definition.$px$ WHERE id = '6bf43c20-223c-467a-b283-5b7cbcefe40d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is A because the theoretical yield must be computed from the limiting reactant before the percent yield can be found, and that chain gives \(80\%\). First convert each charge to moles: \(14.58\,\text{g} \div 24.3\,\text{g/mol} = 0.600\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{Mg}\) and \(9.80\,\text{g} \div 28.0\,\text{g/mol} = 0.350\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{N}_2\). Dividing each by its coefficient in \(3\,\text{Mg} + \text{N}_2 \rightarrow \text{Mg}_3\text{N}_2\) gives \(0.600 \div 3 = 0.200\) for \(\text{Mg}\) versus \(0.350 \div 1 = 0.350\) for \(\text{N}_2\); the smaller value identifies \(\text{Mg}\) as the limiting reactant. The \(3:1\) ratio of \(\text{Mg}\) to \(\text{Mg}_3\text{N}_2\) then sets the theoretical amount at \(0.600 \div 3 = 0.200\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{Mg}_3\text{N}_2\), or \(0.200\,\text{mol} \times 100.9\,\text{g/mol} = 20.18\,\text{g}\). The percent yield is the actual mass over this theoretical mass: \(16.14\,\text{g} \div 20.18\,\text{g} \times 100\% \approx 80\%\). (Choice B) Mis-assigning \(\text{N}_2\) as limiting (for example by comparing raw moles, \(0.350\) versus the unscaled \(0.600\), or by choosing the larger starting mass) gives \(0.350\,\text{mol} \times 100.9 = 35.3\,\text{g}\) theoretical and \(16.14 \div 35.3 \approx 46\%\); this reverses the limiting-reactant result because it omits the coefficients. (Choice C) Carrying \(\text{Mg}\) forward as the limiting reactant but skipping the \(3:1\) ratio, treating \(0.600\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{Mg}\) as \(0.600\,\text{mol}\) \(\text{Mg}_3\text{N}_2\) (\(60.5\,\text{g}\) theoretical), inflates the denominator and yields \(16.14 \div 60.5 \approx 27\%\). (Choice D) Computing the theoretical and actual masses correctly but inverting the percent-yield ratio (\(20.18 \div 16.14 \times 100\%\)) returns \(125\%\), a value above \(100\%\) that is physically impossible for a yield and signals the ratio was flipped. This is a Data-Based and Statistical Reasoning question because you must chain the limiting-reactant determination into a theoretical-yield calculation and then into a percent-yield ratio, carrying each intermediate result into the next step.$px$ WHERE id = '6c14916e-4d44-4cf1-8242-7b5415440d92';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.' When more than one skeletal arrangement is possible, formal charge is the tool used to decide which Lewis structure is most reasonable: the preferred structure minimizes the number of atoms bearing nonzero formal charge, keeps any nonzero charges as small as possible, and places negative formal charge on the most electronegative atom. Formal charge is calculated as (valence electrons) − (nonbonding electrons) − (number of bonds), and the formal charges must sum to the ion's overall charge of \(-1\). (Choice A) is correct: with carbon (the least electronegative of the three) central, the structure \([\text{O}=\text{C}=\text{N}]^-\) leaves only one atom with a nonzero formal charge, that charge is only \(-1\), and it sits on nitrogen/oxygen rather than carbon, satisfying all of the preference guidelines. This is also the arrangement isoelectronic with \(\text{CO}_2\), a well-behaved linear triatomic. (Choice B) is incorrect: putting nitrogen in the center forces a central \(+1\) plus a large \(-2\) onto the terminal carbon, giving two atoms with nonzero formal charge, a much less favorable distribution than a single \(-1\). (Choice C) is incorrect: once octets are filled, a central oxygen is forced to \(+2\) (a large-magnitude positive charge stranded on the most electronegative atom), with the two terminal atoms carrying \(-2\) and \(-1\); this places three atoms at nonzero charge and directly violates the guideline that negative charge belongs on the most electronegative atom. (Choice D) is incorrect: even with the correct carbon-central skeleton, deliberately assigning the \(-1\) to carbon rather than to the more electronegative terminal atom gives a worse formal-charge distribution and is not what the octet-filled structure actually yields. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the formal-charge guidelines to compare candidate structures and identify the one with the most favorable charge distribution.$px$ WHERE id = '6c4038e2-32b7-4ea4-8196-f2138562c76c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' Bone's rigid extracellular matrix has two structural contributors that divide the mechanical labor: inorganic hydroxyapatite crystals (a form of calcium phosphate) and organic collagen fibers. The hydroxyapatite crystals give bone its hardness and strength, while the collagen fibers give it flexibility so that it is not brittle; without the mineral crystals bone would flex and provide little support, whereas without collagen bone would be brittle and shatter easily. A dilute acid dissolves the inorganic mineral (calcium salts are acid-soluble) but spares the collagen, so what remains is the flexible protein scaffold. (Choice A) is correct: dissolving the hydroxyapatite strips away the mineral that normally supplies hardness and rigidity, leaving only the collagen scaffold, which is why the demineralized bone bends and can be knotted instead of holding its shape. (Choice B) is incorrect: acid dissolves mineral salts, not the protein, so collagen is the component that remains, not the one lost; it also misassigns hardness to collagen, when hardness comes from the mineral. (Choice C) is incorrect: it correctly identifies hydroxyapatite as the component removed by acid but assigns it the wrong mechanical role. The mineral provides hardness and rigidity, whereas flexibility and resistance to shattering come from collagen. (Choice D) is incorrect: acid does not remove collagen, and even if it did, loss of the flexible component would make the bone more brittle and rigid, which is the opposite of the soft, bendable result observed. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinct mechanical roles of the inorganic and organic matrix components to predict which component acid removes and how its loss changes the bone's behavior.$px$ WHERE id = '6c6035b2-683f-488f-a6bf-fae0bf9e979d';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is B because the D/L configuration of a sugar is defined by the orientation of the hydroxyl group on the chiral center most remote from the carbonyl carbon, which in an aldohexose is C5, the highest-numbered stereocenter (the terminal CH2OH carbon, C6, is not a stereocenter). Reading the Fischer projection shown, the OH on that carbon extends to the right of the vertical backbone. By convention every sugar is referenced to glyceraldehyde: when the OH on the last (highest-numbered) chiral carbon points right, as in D-glyceraldehyde, the sugar is assigned the D configuration. Extracting that single orientation from the figure and mapping it to the reference rule yields D.

(Choice B) is correct: it identifies the correct reference carbon (the highest-numbered chiral center) and applies the correct rule (OH on the right of the Fischer projection at that carbon defines D).

(Choice A) is incorrect: it inspects the correct carbon but reverses the rule. An OH pointing to the right at the highest-numbered chiral center defines D, not L; L is assigned only when that OH points to the left (\(L =\) Last carbon, Left OH). This is the reversed-relationship trap.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it lands on the D configuration but justifies it using the wrong stereocenter, the carbon adjacent to the carbonyl (C2), which is the chiral center nearest the aldehyde. D/L is set by the most remote chiral center, not the nearest one; using C2 would misassign many sugars (for example, sugars that are epimeric at an internal carbon), so the reasoning is invalid even though the label happens to match.

(Choice D) is incorrect: it invokes the anomeric carbon, which does not exist in the open-chain Fischer form shown and, when the ring does close, governs the \(\alpha/\beta\) designation, not the D/L designation. Anomers differ only at the hemiacetal/acetal carbon; that distinction is unrelated to absolute D/L configuration.

Skill 2: this item requires applying the D/L assignment convention to a specific structure. You must locate the correct diagnostic carbon in the figure, read the orientation of its OH, and connect that observation to the glyceraldehyde reference rule rather than recalling a memorized fact.$px$ WHERE id = '6c9447f7-b862-4af5-a23a-521977dea374';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is D because anaerobic muscle makes ATP by glycolysis but must reoxidize NADH to NAD+ to keep going, which it does by reducing pyruvate to lactate; shipping that lactate to the liver lets the liver rebuild glucose by gluconeogenesis, a process that costs \(6\) ATP per glucose versus the \(2\) ATP glycolysis produced, so the cycle is net ATP-consuming and the liver pays that bill.

(Choice D) is correct: muscle offloads lactate and regenerates NAD+ to sustain glycolysis, and the liver spends the ATP to remake glucose, placing the energy cost on the liver.

(Choice A) is incorrect: the Cori cycle does not create energy. Hepatic gluconeogenesis consumes more ATP than muscle glycolysis released, so the cycle is a net drain, not a net gain.

(Choice B) is incorrect: lactate carries no amino nitrogen; transferring nitrogen for urea synthesis while making glucose describes the glucose-alanine cycle, not the Cori cycle.

(Choice C) is incorrect: the liver does not oxidize the lactate to CO2 for ATP. It runs gluconeogenesis to regenerate glucose, and the cost is not shared equally but falls on the liver.

This question tests Skill 2 (Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving) by asking you to trace the redox and energy logic linking two organs rather than recall the cycle's name.$px$ WHERE id = '6d13d675-9f7f-435b-be84-494da6c57fb0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' The modern periodic law states that the chemical and physical properties of the elements are periodic functions of their atomic numbers \(Z\) (the proton count), not their atomic masses. Tellurium has a larger atomic mass (\(\approx 127.6\)) than iodine (\(\approx 126.9\)), so a strictly mass-ordered table would invert the two. Ordering instead by atomic number places \(\text{Te}\) (\(Z = 52\)) before \(\text{I}\) (\(Z = 53\)), which correctly groups \(\text{I}\) with the other halogens and \(\text{Te}\) with the other chalcogens, matching their observed chemistry; this is why the \(Z\)-based law supersedes Mendeleev's original mass-based arrangement, making A correct. (Choice B) This reverses the modern periodic law: arranging by increasing atomic mass is precisely what produces the \(\text{Te}\)-\(\text{I}\) inversion and the mismatch with chemical behavior, so atomic mass is the discarded ordering criterion, not the governing one. (Choice C) Atomic mass does not increase monotonically across every period; the \(\text{Te}\)/\(\text{I}\) pair (along with \(\text{Ar}\)/\(\text{K}\) and \(\text{Co}\)/\(\text{Ni}\)) is a documented mass inversion, so position cannot be fixed by requiring mass to rise steadily. (Choice D) Periodic recurrence tracks atomic number, the proton count that determines electron configuration; neutron number varies among isotopes of one element and does not set an element's place in the table or the recurrence of its properties. Skill 1: this question requires recall and identification of the foundational statement of the periodic law.$px$ WHERE id = '6d14a4f7-c792-4536-bad3-0a45a63a11f9';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)'. The answer is D because in a population fixed at random moments, the fraction of cells caught in any phase is proportional to how long that phase lasts, and interphase (G1 + S + G2 = 9 + 10 + 4.5 = 23.5 hours) consumes nearly the entire 24-hour cycle while the M phase lasts only about half an hour. The text states that "the G1 phase lasts approximately nine hours, the S phase lasts 10 hours, the G2 phase lasts about four and one-half hours, and the M phase lasts approximately one-half hour," and it defines the three stages of interphase as G1, S, and G2. Because roughly 23.5 of every 24 hours are spent in interphase, a random snapshot will almost always land there. (Choice A) Metaphase is when chromosomes are "maximally condensed" and thus most visible, but visibility is not duration. Mitosis as a whole occupies only about half an hour, so a random cell is rarely caught at metaphase. (Choice B) Prophase opens the brief ~0.5-hour M phase; treating mitosis as the time-dominant part of the cycle overestimates how long cells spend there. (Choice C) S phase is the single longest individual step at 10 hours, but it is only one stage of interphase; all of interphase (23.5 hours) is far more probable than S phase alone. (Choice D) Interphase spans G1, S, and G2, totaling 23.5 of 24 hours, so it is overwhelmingly the most likely phase to catch at random. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires converting per-phase durations into relative probabilities and inferring which phase a random sample most likely represents.$px$ WHERE id = '6d856d69-d57d-4b48-8cd4-f484c3de6e48';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO)'. The answer is A because S phase doubles the nuclear DNA, yet each replicated chromosome remains a single chromosome consisting of two sister chromatids held together at one centromere, so the chromosome number does not change in G2. The text states that "In the S phase, DNA replication can proceed through the mechanisms that result in the formation of identical pairs of DNA molecules (sister chromatids) that are firmly attached at the centromeric region," and that "After replication, the chromosomes are composed of two linked sister chromatids... The connection between the sister chromatids is closest in a region called the centromere." Because the two chromatids stay joined at one centromere, the structure is still counted as one chromosome; the count of 46 is therefore unchanged in G2 while the DNA quantity has doubled. Only at anaphase does this change, when "the cohesin proteins degrade, and the sister chromatids separate at the centromere. Each chromatid, now called a single chromosome," doubling the count after G2. (Choice A) is correct: doubled DNA with an unchanged chromosome number of 46 reflects 46 two-chromatid chromosomes still joined at single centromeres. (Choice B) is wrong because replication does not create separate chromosomes; it makes sister chromatids that share one centromere, so the number stays 46 even though DNA doubles. (Choice C) reverses the relationship, claiming DNA is constant while chromosome number doubles, but S phase is precisely the synthesis of DNA, and the chromatids remain joined as one chromosome. (Choice D) describes the anaphase outcome, where cohesin degrades and separated chromatids each become a chromosome (count 92); that event has not occurred in the G2 state being asked about. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires chaining the molecular event of S-phase replication to the centromere-joined sister-chromatid architecture in order to predict, for a specific G2 cell, how DNA amount and chromosome number diverge.$px$ WHERE id = '6d871d32-6a1c-47c1-95f2-ff1c18b569c5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' An \(\alpha\)-helix is held together by regular backbone hydrogen bonds between each residue's amide N-H (donor) and a carbonyl oxygen four residues away (acceptor). Because proline's side chain loops back and bonds to its own \(\alpha\)-amino nitrogen, that nitrogen sits in the ring as a secondary amine and lacks the free backbone N-H the helix needs; proline is also sterically locked into a limited backbone conformation, so it cannot adopt the helical geometry. (Choice A) is correct: proline's ring side chain cannot provide the backbone hydrogen bond needed for helix stabilization and is sterically locked into a single conformation, so the missing backbone hydrogen-bond donor is exactly what interrupts the helix. (Choice B) is incorrect: proline's cyclic side chain is actually compact rather than bulky, and helix breakage arises from the lost backbone N-H and conformational rigidity, not from side-chain steric clashes with neighbors. (Choice C) is incorrect: proline is a nonpolar residue with an uncharged side chain, so it carries no charge to electrostatically repel backbone groups. (Choice D) is incorrect: this reverses the true relationship. Proline removes a backbone hydrogen-bond donor rather than adding side-chain hydrogen bonds, so it loosens rather than over-tightens the helix. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply proline's unique ring structure to predict its mechanistic effect on \(\alpha\)-helix hydrogen bonding in a novel substitution experiment.$px$ WHERE id = '6d9b99b8-a57e-469c-9e60-9f2f23d76649';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is B because the woman is a heterozygous carrier (\(Aa\)) who is phenotypically unaffected yet still carries one recessive allele that she can pass to a gamete and thus to her children. A heterozygous individual who carries a recessive allele but does not express it is referred to as a carrier; the recessive allele is \"carried\" and not expressed because, in the presence of the dominant allele, the recessive allele is hidden and makes no contribution to the phenotype. Because each gamete receives only one of the two alleles, a carrier has a 50% chance of passing the recessive allele to any given child, even though she herself shows the dominant (unaffected) phenotype. Only statement III is therefore accurate. (Choice A) I only is wrong because statement I is false: a simple recessive carrier does NOT show an intermediate phenotype; an intermediate (blended) phenotype is the signature of incomplete dominance, a separate inheritance pattern in which the heterozygote displays a phenotype between the two homozygotes. (Choice B) is correct: statement III alone is accurate because a phenotypically unaffected heterozygote still harbors the recessive allele and, by the law of segregation, can transmit it to offspring. (Choice C) I and III only is wrong because it incorrectly accepts statement I; the carrier looks identical to a homozygous dominant individual, not intermediate. (Choice D) II and III only is wrong because it incorrectly accepts statement II; a carrier is heterozygous (\(Aa\)), not homozygous recessive (\(aa\)). A homozygous recessive individual would actually be affected by the disease. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that a heterozygous carrier shows the dominant phenotype while still transmitting the recessive allele.$px$ WHERE id = '6e21e5fb-57c1-455e-a00d-0299eca0d2d0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Nervous System Organization'. The answer is A because the sympathetic ('fight or flight') response dilates the airways to raise oxygen intake during stress. The physiological effects of sympathetic norepinephrine release include dilating the trachea and bronchi to make breathing easier, increasing heart rate, and shifting blood away from the skin toward the heart, muscles, and brain. Widening the bronchi reduces airway resistance so the fleeing gazelle can take in more air and oxygenate its blood for sustained running. (Choice A) is correct: sympathetic activation relaxes bronchial smooth muscle, dilating the bronchi to widen the airways. (Choice B) is the opposite, parasympathetic, effect. The vagus nerve constricts the bronchial tubes in the lungs, whereas the sympathetic system does not. (Choice C) describes a parasympathetic 'rest and digest' effect; sympathetic activation inhibits digestion, whereas parasympathetic acetylcholine stimulates digestion. (Choice D) reverses the sympathetic effect on the heart, which actually increases heart rate rather than slowing it. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires recalling the established sympathetic effect on the airways rather than reasoning from supplied data.$px$ WHERE id = '6e2745f3-a06c-4e0a-b57a-cfc388b8dabf';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is an Organic Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure, function, and reactivity of biologically relevant molecules'. The answer to this question is D because acetone (propanone) is \(\text{CH}_3\text{COCH}_3\): its central carbonyl carbon is flanked by two methyl groups, so the \(\text{C=O}\) sits internally between two carbons (the defining arrangement of a ketone), and with only three carbons it is the smallest structure meeting that requirement. (Choice A) \(\text{HCHO}\) (formaldehyde) has a carbonyl carbon bearing two hydrogens and no carbon neighbor, so its \(\text{C=O}\) is a terminal aldehyde, not an internal ketone carbonyl. (Choice B) \(\text{CH}_3\text{CHO}\) (acetaldehyde) carries its \(\text{C=O}\) at the chain end with one hydrogen on the carbonyl carbon, making it an aldehyde rather than a ketone. (Choice C) \(\text{CH}_3\text{COCH}_2\text{CH}_3\) (butanone) is a genuine ketone (its carbonyl carbon does bond two carbons), but its four-carbon chain makes it larger than acetone, so it is not the simplest ketone. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because it requires connecting the common name acetone to its condensed structure and recognizing the internal-carbonyl arrangement that defines a ketone.$px$ WHERE id = '6e4d9d26-ebd0-460e-90ec-b9a258287b49';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because functional residual capacity (FRC) is the volume remaining in the lungs after a normal, passive expiration, equal to expiratory reserve volume (ERV) plus residual volume (RV). RV is not measured directly by spirometry, so it must first be derived: total lung capacity (TLC) = tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume + expiratory reserve volume + residual volume, giving RV = 6000 − (500 + 3100 + 1200) = 1200 mL. FRC = ERV + RV = 1200 + 1200 = 2400 mL. (Choice A) is incorrect: 1200 mL is a single component: the ERV alone, which also happens to equal the derived RV alone. Reporting one component omits the other pool of air, and FRC requires summing both the ERV and the residual air that remain in the lungs after a quiet expiration. (Choice C) is correct: RV is recovered as 1200 mL from TLC minus the three measured volumes, and adding it to the 1200 mL ERV yields the 2400 mL FRC. (Choice B) is incorrect: 2900 mL = ERV + RV + tidal volume (1200 + 1200 + 500) improperly folds the tidal volume into the capacity; the 500 mL tidal breath is exhaled during the passive expiration itself, so it is not part of the air still retained after that expiration and must not be added to FRC. (Choice D) is incorrect: 3600 mL = tidal volume + inspiratory reserve volume is the inspiratory capacity (IC), the maximal volume that can be inhaled from the end of a quiet expiration; this confuses the volume that can be drawn in with FRC, the volume left behind, so it sums the wrong two components entirely. This item tests the skill of reasoning through multi-step quantitative data to derive an unmeasured value and combine components into a defined respiratory capacity.$px$ WHERE id = '6eb33315-464a-4912-aab2-eb24f2ca81af';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The two binding models differ directly: in the lock-and-key model the reactant fits the exact shape of the enzyme's binding site, and substrates bind without any further modification to the enzyme's shape, whereas in the induced-fit model contact between the reactant and the protein changes the protein's shape so that it can bind, adjusting to better fit the substrate. Locking the pocket into its substrate-bound conformation while keeping it complementary to the free substrate converts the enzyme from induced-fit behavior to lock-and-key behavior: the complementary shape is now pre-formed, so the substrate can still fit, but the enzyme no longer undergoes a conformational change when the substrate contacts it. (Choice A) is correct: because the pocket is pre-shaped to match the free substrate, binding can still occur, but the defining induced-fit event (a shape change on substrate contact) is eliminated, which is exactly the lock-and-key behavior in which binding occurs without any further modification to the enzyme's shape. (Choice B) is incorrect: it invents refolding before the substrate approaches; the induced-fit change occurs at the moment of contact, when that contact changes the protein's shape, not as repeated pre-binding refolding, and a pocket already complementary to the free substrate has no need to refold to admit it. (Choice C) is incorrect: it misplaces the conformational change onto the substrate; the change is in the protein's shape (the enzyme's shape), not the substrate, so this reflects the common error of thinking the substrate remodels to the enzyme rather than the enzyme remodeling to the substrate. (Choice D) is incorrect: it assumes a rigid pocket cannot bind, but a rigid pocket already complementary to the substrate is precisely the lock-and-key situation in which the reactant fits the exact shape of the enzyme's binding site. Rigidity does not prevent binding when the shape already matches. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the defining contrast between the induced-fit and lock-and-key models to predict how removing the substrate-induced conformational change alters an enzyme's binding behavior.$px$ WHERE id = '6ef19648-d986-4e1c-b959-75a87c920c92';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A geneticist studying three linked loci in a fungus measures the following pairwise recombination distances: genes \(A\) and \(B\) are \(8\ \text{cM}\) apart, genes \(B\) and \(C\) are \(12\ \text{cM}\) apart, and genes \(A\) and \(C\) are \(20\ \text{cM}\) apart. On the basis of these data, which of the following statements about the arrangement of the three genes is accurate?

I. Gene \(B\) lies between genes \(A\) and \(C\).
II. Gene \(A\) lies between genes \(B\) and \(C\).
III. The \(A\)-\(C\) distance equals the sum of the \(A\)-\(B\) and \(B\)-\(C\) distances.$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is C because the largest of the three pairwise distances flanks the remaining gene, and because map distances are additive along a chromosome. Map distances in centimorgans correspond to recombination frequencies, which allows distances between several genes on the same chromosome to be calculated additively; because these distances combine by addition and subtraction, separations along a chromosome are simply summed or differenced. Here the largest distance is \(A\)-\(C = 20\ \text{cM}\), so \(A\) and \(C\) flank the third gene; placing \(B\) between them gives the order \(A\)-\(B\)-\(C\), and \(8\ \text{cM} + 12\ \text{cM} = 20\ \text{cM}\) confirms it. Thus statement I (\(B\) is the middle gene) and statement III (the two short distances sum to the long one) are both true, while statement II places an end gene in the middle and is false. (Choice A) I only is a partial truth: it correctly puts \(B\) in the middle but ignores the additive relationship the data themselves verify, since \(8 + 12 = 20\). (Choice B) III only confirms the arithmetic yet wrongly treats the gene order as undetermined, even though the largest distance unambiguously identifies \(A\) and \(C\) as the flanking genes. (Choice C) is correct: \(B\) is flanked by \(A\) and \(C\), giving order \(A\)-\(B\)-\(C\), and \(20\ \text{cM} = 8\ \text{cM} + 12\ \text{cM}\). (Choice D) II and III only reverses the placement rule, putting the terminal gene \(A\) in the middle; the gene that lies between the others is the one NOT appearing in the largest pairwise distance, which is \(B\), not \(A\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the additivity of map distances to deduce gene order from three recombination measurements.$px$ WHERE id = '6f0fc7f5-078a-435c-a2df-825ef6de2acf';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because whether actin-binding sites are exposed is set solely by the position of tropomyosin, which is held away from those sites as long as \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-bound troponin keeps it shifted. When \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) binds troponin, tropomyosin slides away from the binding sites on the actin strands, allowing the myosin heads to bind those exposed sites; reshielding occurs only once \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is pumped back into the SR, causing tropomyosin to recover the sites. Because \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is still bound to troponin here, tropomyosin stays off the sites and the sites stay exposed regardless of the \(\text{ATP}\) level. The separate fact that the cross-bridges cannot release is governed by \(\text{ATP}\): in its absence the myosin head cannot detach from actin, producing rigor. That controls bridge detachment, not the exposure of the actin sites themselves. (Choice A) Absence of \(\text{ATP}\) does explain why the formed cross-bridges stay locked, but it does not determine whether the underlying actin sites are exposed; site exposure is a tropomyosin-position question, not a myosin-detachment question. (Choice B) Pump failure keeping \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) in the sarcoplasm is the upstream reason \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is still on troponin, but the immediate gating variable for site exposure is tropomyosin's position, the step the persisting \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-troponin binding acts through. (Choice C) Correct: tropomyosin position is the direct gating variable for actin-site exposure, and \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-bound troponin keeps tropomyosin displaced, so as long as \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) remains on troponin the sites stay uncovered. (Choice D) Re-cocking the myosin head via \(\text{ATP}\) hydrolysis is part of the cross-bridge cycle and is blocked when \(\text{ATP}\) is absent, but re-cocking is irrelevant to whether tropomyosin uncovers the actin sites. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that tropomyosin position, controlled by \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-bound troponin, is the variable that gates actin-site exposure, distinct from the \(\text{ATP}\)-dependent detachment that fails in rigor.$px$ WHERE id = '6f26a6dd-d978-4856-8eaa-ec50a5584a64';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Mechanisms of Hormone Action.'

The answer is C because the endpoint of the cAMP second-messenger cascade is the phosphorylation of target enzymes by activated protein kinase A (PKA), and that phosphorylation carries out the change specified by the hormone. In a fasting liver cell, the peptide hormone acting through this pathway (Gs to adenylyl cyclase to cAMP to PKA) is glucagon, whose PKA-driven phosphorylation of metabolic enzymes stimulates the breakdown of glycogen stores, releasing glucose and raising blood glucose. The scenario supplies only the cascade mechanics, so the student must trace the phosphorylation step to its metabolic consequence rather than restate a definition.

(Choice C) is correct: PKA phosphorylation of the liver cell's enzymes activates glycogen breakdown (glycogenolysis), releasing glucose. That is the functional endpoint the glucagon-driven cAMP cascade produces.

(Choice B) is incorrect: building glycogen to store glucose is the antagonistic action of insulin, which lowers blood glucose; it is the opposite of what a cAMP-PKA phosphorylation cascade in a fasting liver cell produces.

(Choice A) is incorrect: suppression of adenylyl cyclase and falling cAMP describe an inhibitory Gi pathway (for example, somatostatin/GHIH), not the stimulatory Gs cascade that has already activated PKA in this scenario.

(Choice D) is incorrect: calcium release from the smooth endoplasmic reticulum is triggered by IP3 in the phospholipase C pathway, a separate second-messenger system, not the cAMP-PKA phosphorylation cascade described here.

At the Skill 2 level, the student cannot readback an answer; they must map the general cascade endpoint (phosphorylation of enzymes by an activated kinase) onto the specific metabolic outcome in the cell described.$px$ WHERE id = '6f6bfce4-99af-4dad-b469-f2229d3668d9';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' Under the sliding filament model, a sarcomere shortens because the thin (actin) and thick (myosin) filaments slide past one another; the filaments themselves do not shorten, only their degree of overlap changes. Because the A band corresponds to the full length of the thick filament, its width is fixed no matter how much the sarcomere contracts, while the I band (thin filament only) and the H zone (thick filament only) shrink as overlap increases and the Z lines are pulled inward. (Choice A) is correct: the A band spans the entire length of the thick filament, and since the thick filament does not change length during the sliding filament mechanism, the A band width stays constant even as the sarcomere shortens. (Choice B) is incorrect: the I band does narrow during contraction because it is the thin-filament-only region, and increasing overlap with the thick filaments reduces it; the thin filament's length is constant, but the I band width is not. (Choice C) is incorrect: it is true that thin filaments do not extend into the H zone at rest, but during contraction the thin filaments slide toward the center and encroach on this region, so the H zone actually narrows rather than staying constant. (Choice D) is incorrect: it names the correct band but gives a false reason. Filaments do not detach across the A band during contraction; cross-bridges cycle there, and the band's constancy reflects the fixed thick-filament length, not detachment. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the sliding filament model to predict how each sarcomere band's dimensions respond to shortening.$px$ WHERE id = '6facf011-1034-4e2e-b710-47c288784ab6';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because the rate a gas diffuses across the moist respiratory membrane depends not only on its partial-pressure gradient but also on how readily it dissolves in the aqueous barrier; CO2 is roughly 20 times more soluble than O2, so many more CO2 molecules enter solution and cross per unit of gradient, allowing CO2 to keep pace despite its ~5 mmHg gradient being far smaller than O2's ~64 mmHg gradient. (Choice D) is correct: CO2's much greater aqueous solubility multiplies its diffusion rate for a given gradient, so its small gradient still moves as much gas as O2's large gradient. (Choice A) is incorrect: heavier gases actually diffuse slightly more slowly (Graham's law relates rate inversely to the square root of molar mass), and molecular momentum is not the operative factor across a liquid barrier; solubility is. (Choice B) is incorrect: diffusion across the membrane is driven by dissolved-gas partial pressures, not by hemoglobin binding, and no gas is pulled across against its own partial-pressure gradient; hemoglobin binding maintains the gradient but does not carry gas through the membrane. (Choice C) is incorrect: most CO2 in blood is in fact carried as bicarbonate and as carbamino compounds, not as dissolved gas, and it is CO2's high solubility, not a steeper gradient, that speeds its membrane crossing. The skill assessed is applying the principle that diffusion rate across the respiratory membrane depends jointly on the partial-pressure gradient and the gas's solubility in the aqueous barrier.$px$ WHERE id = '7006c8e7-74ee-4030-adbc-4d2b18379575';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure.' The periodic table sorts elements into three broad classes by their bundle of physical properties: metals are shiny, malleable (deform without breaking), and good conductors of heat and electricity; nonmetals are dull, brittle, and poor conductors; and metalloids fall in between, conducting only moderately well and behaving as semiconductors. To classify element X you must match its full property profile against these classes rather than any single clue. (Choice C) is correct: element X is brittle (a nonmetal-like trait), yet conducts electricity moderately (better than the nonmetal \(\text{S}\) but far worse than the metal \(\text{Cu}\)) and serves as a semiconductor, which is precisely the intermediate metal/nonmetal behavior that defines a metalloid such as \(\text{Si}\) or \(\text{Ge}\). (Choice A) is incorrect: a metal would be malleable rather than brittle and would conduct electricity nearly as well as \(\text{Cu}\), not far more poorly, so element X fails the defining conductivity and mechanical tests for a metal. (Choice B) is incorrect: element X is brittle like a nonmetal, but a true nonmetal is a poor conductor comparable to \(\text{S}\); because X conducts substantially better than \(\text{S}\) and functions as a semiconductor, it lies above the nonmetal class in conductivity. (Choice D) is incorrect: transition metals are a subset of the metals and share metallic malleability and high conductivity, so a brittle, only-moderately-conducting semiconductor cannot belong to this group. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the property criteria that distinguish metals, nonmetals, and metalloids to an unknown element's measured behavior rather than recall a single definition.$px$ WHERE id = '70281532-f3a1-417a-b314-0803bc553d5c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is D because polysaccharides are grouped by how many kinds of monosaccharide they contain, not by whether they are branched. Since complete hydrolysis of glycogen releases glucose as the only monosaccharide, glycogen is assembled from a single type of monomer and is therefore a homopolysaccharide (like starch, cellulose, and chitin).

(Choice A) is incorrect: a heteropolysaccharide is built from more than one kind of monomer (e.g., the repeating GlcNAc-uronic-acid disaccharides of glycosaminoglycans). Glycogen's branching makes it structurally elaborate, but branching does not add a second monomer type. Every residue is still glucose, so it is not a heteropolysaccharide.

(Choice B) is incorrect: glycosaminoglycans (e.g., hyaluronate, chondroitin sulfate) are heteropolysaccharides with a repeating disaccharide that includes an amino sugar, and they are structural/extracellular molecules rather than fuel stores. Glycogen contains only glucose and is not a GAG.

(Choice D) is correct: a polymer made from a single kind of monosaccharide is a homopolysaccharide, and glycogen releasing only glucose fits that definition exactly.

(Choice C) is incorrect: "oligosaccharide" describes chain length (a short chain of only a few sugars, as in \(N\)-linked glycans), not monomer diversity. Glycogen is a very large, many-thousand-residue polysaccharide, so this length-based category does not apply.

This is a Skill 1 (Knowledge of Scientific Concepts) item: it asks you to apply the definition of a homopolysaccharide to a polymer described by its monomer composition.$px$ WHERE id = '70d46568-7314-45bc-b7ce-34da30cc709b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is D because a pyranose ring is a cyclic hemiacetal that can reversibly open to the open-chain aldehyde form. When pure α-D-glucopyranose dissolves, its anomeric hemiacetal transiently opens to the achiral-at-C1 straight-chain aldehyde; re-closure can generate either the α or the β anomer. Given enough time, the solution reaches an equilibrium mixture that contains both anomers in a fixed, thermodynamically determined ratio (one anomer is somewhat favored, but neither is eliminated). The changing optical rotation that settles to a constant value is the signature of this interconversion.

(Choice D) is correct: it captures both features of the equilibrium: both anomers coexist, and their ratio is fixed and unequal because the two anomers differ slightly in stability.

(Choice B) is incorrect: it assumes the anomeric center is locked. It is a hemiacetal, not a full acetal, so no enzyme or added reagent is needed for the ring to open and re-close; the optical rotation would not drift and then stabilize if nothing were interconverting.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it confuses "one anomer is favored at equilibrium" with "the reaction goes to completion." Equilibrium between two species of comparable free energy leaves a measurable amount of both; the α form is not fully consumed.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it confuses anomerization with epimerization at a different carbon. Ring opening and re-closure only changes the configuration at the anomeric carbon (C1), producing the other anomer of the same sugar. It does not invert C4 to convert glucose into galactose.

At the Skill 2 level, answering requires reasoning from the hemiacetal ring-opening mechanism to predict the equilibrium state, rather than recalling a stated definition: you must recognize that reversible opening scrambles only C1, so a single pure anomer must relax to a two-anomer mixture of the same sugar.$px$ WHERE id = '70dfaf69-8252-4f21-ae23-5c77e18c6748';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers.' During organogenesis, the ectoderm gives rise to both the outer epidermis and the neural tissue of the central nervous system, but these are not equivalent default fates: growth factors signal some cells at the edge of the ectoderm to become epidermis cells, while the remaining cells in the center form the neural plate, and if this growth-factor signaling were disrupted, the entire ectoderm would differentiate into neural tissue. In other words, neural is the default ectodermal fate, and an active growth-factor signal is required to divert edge cells into the epidermal pathway. (Choice A) is correct: removing the growth-factor signal from the entire ectoderm removes the only cue that produces epidermis, so all ectodermal cells follow the default neural pathway and no epidermis forms. (Choice B) is incorrect because it reverses the roles of signal and default; epidermis is the induced fate that requires the growth-factor signal, so eliminating the signal abolishes epidermis rather than producing it everywhere. (Choice C) is incorrect because normal proportions of epidermis and neural tissue depend on the spatial restriction of the growth-factor signal to edge cells; with the signal gone entirely, the balance collapses toward neural tissue rather than remaining unchanged. (Choice D) is incorrect because neural tissue is an ectodermal derivative, not a mesodermal one. The mesoderm flanking the neural tube gives rise to connective tissues, somites, and the notochord, not to the neural plate. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rule that neural tissue is the default ectodermal fate and epidermis is the signal-induced fate to predict the outcome of removing that inductive signal.$px$ WHERE id = '710e4769-213d-415f-ab80-c13bbb7d8fb5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO).' Prokaryotes are metabolically diverse: their energy-producing pathways may be aerobic, using oxygen as the terminal electron acceptor, or anaerobic, using an inorganic or organic molecule as the terminal electron acceptor. In anaerobic cellular respiration the electron transport chain still operates, but it passes electrons to an inorganic final acceptor; sulfate-reducing bacteria, for example, reduce sulfate to hydrogen sulfide to regenerate NAD+ from NADH. (Choice A) is correct: the culture grows without oxygen, generates hydrogen sulfide from sulfate, and stops growing when the electron transport chain is blocked, which together indicate a functioning chain that terminates on sulfate as an inorganic electron acceptor. That is the defining feature of anaerobic respiration. (Choice B) is incorrect: fermentation regenerates NAD+ by handing electrons to an organic molecule and uses no electron transport chain, so a fermenting cell would be unaffected by an inhibitor of chain electron flow, contradicting the observation. (Choice C) is incorrect: aerobic respiration is defined by oxygen serving as the terminal electron acceptor, and this vessel contains no oxygen; sulfate is not simply plugged into an oxygen-requiring aerobic pathway but is the acceptor of a distinct anaerobic one. (Choice D) is incorrect: relying solely on substrate-level phosphorylation in glycolysis would not require a membrane electron transport chain, so blocking that chain would not abolish growth as it does here. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of aerobic respiration, anaerobic respiration, and fermentation to the experimental observations and infer which pathway is consistent with growth without oxygen, sulfide production, and dependence on an intact electron transport chain.$px$ WHERE id = '710f7b2b-71fa-47f8-875f-04d88ae106cb';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is B because the pancreatic islet places two glucose-responsive cell types side by side and wires them to the same signal in opposite directions. As blood glucose falls across the fed-to-fasted transition, the beta cells sense the drop and cut insulin secretion, while the alpha cells respond to that same fall by increasing glucagon secretion. Because one input moves the two hormones in opposite directions, the insulin-to-glucagon ratio swings sharply and unambiguously toward glucagon. It is that ratio, not either hormone alone, that flips the liver out of anabolic storage and into catabolic mobilization. This reciprocal, ratio-based readout of a single blood-glucose signal is exactly what lets the islet serve as the body's master fed-versus-fasted sensor.

(Choice B) is correct: the same falling glucose lowers beta-cell insulin and raises alpha-cell glucagon, so one signal drives the insulin-to-glucagon ratio toward glucagon and switches the liver from storage to output.

(Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the direction of both hormones. In the fed state insulin is high and glucagon low; as glucose FALLS insulin drops and glucagon rises, so the fasting signal is a FALLING insulin-to-glucagon ratio. A rising ratio would command storage, not mobilization.

(Choice C) is incorrect: alpha and beta cells do not both increase their output together. If they did, the two opposing hormones would tend to cancel. Their reciprocal movement (insulin down, glucagon up) is precisely what makes the ratio, rather than the total amount of islet hormone, the meaningful fed/fasted signal.

(Choice D) is incorrect: insulin withdrawal is only half of the switch. Alpha cells actively raise glucagon as glucose falls, and this reciprocal rise is needed to drive hepatic glucose output, so glucagon is not a passive constant across the transition.

This question rewards Skill 2 (reasoning): you had to see that two cell types reading one glucose signal in opposite directions turn the insulin-to-glucagon ratio into a bidirectional fed/fasted switch, rather than recall a single hormone's action.$px$, options = $px$[{"label":"A","text":"As glucose falls, beta cells instead raise their insulin output while alpha cells lower their glucagon output, so it is a rising insulin-to-glucagon ratio that commands the liver to switch over to mobilizing its stored fuel."},{"label":"B","text":"The same falling glucose drives the two cell types oppositely (beta-cell insulin falls while alpha-cell glucagon rises), so one signal swings the insulin-to-glucagon ratio toward glucagon and flips the liver from storage to output."},{"label":"C","text":"Falling glucose makes both alpha and beta cells secrete more at once, so insulin and glucagon climb together, and it is this rise in the islet's total hormone output, rather than any change in their ratio, that registers the onset of the fast."},{"label":"D","text":"Only the beta cells actually sense glucose; alpha-cell glucagon holds essentially constant across the transition, so the shift toward mobilization is produced entirely by the withdrawal of insulin, with the reciprocal rise in glucagon contributing nothing."}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = '712699c8-5c55-427d-9871-fdb01819b059';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Embryogenesis & Development.' During gastrulation, an embryo establishes three primary germ layers, and each is programmed to give rise to specific tissues: the ectoderm forms the epidermis and central nervous system, the endoderm forms the linings of the digestive and respiratory tracts, and the mesoderm forms all specialized muscle tissue, connective tissues such as the skeleton and blood, and most visceral organs. The task requires tracing several affected structures back to a single common origin rather than recalling one structure-to-layer pairing. (Choice B) is correct: cardiac muscle, the kidneys, and the bony skeleton all arise from mesoderm, which produces specialized muscle, connective tissue (including bone), and most visceral organs, so blocking mesoderm alone accounts for exactly this cluster of defects while sparing the epidermis and gut lining. (Choice A) is incorrect: although the kidneys are internal organs, endoderm does not give rise to visceral organs generally (it forms epithelial linings), so this describes the wrong layer, and the intact gut lining actually argues against endoderm damage. (Choice C) is incorrect: ectoderm forms the epidermis and nervous system, but the epidermis developed normally here, and ectoderm does not produce cardiac muscle, kidneys, or bone. (Choice D) is incorrect: endoderm does form the digestive and respiratory linings, but those structures were spared, and endoderm produces none of the muscle, renal, or skeletal tissues that were lost. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply germ-layer derivation rules to a novel injury pattern and identify the single layer whose loss explains all of the observed defects while sparing the unaffected tissues.$px$ WHERE id = '719a55f6-3592-4f66-a8b7-4449c700e235';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because compliance is the change in lung volume produced per unit change in transpulmonary (distending) pressure; a fibrotic lung has low compliance, so a larger transpulmonary pressure change is needed to add each unit of volume, and since alveolar pressure returns to near-atmospheric while air flows, that larger transpulmonary pressure must come from a larger (more negative) drop in intrapleural pressure to achieve the same 0.5 L tidal volume. (Choice D) is correct: it correctly pairs the larger intrapleural pressure drop with the mechanism that a stiffer, low-compliance lung requires more transpulmonary pressure per unit volume. (Choice A) is incorrect: although its stated mechanism (that a stiffer lung needs a greater transpulmonary pressure) is accurate, the magnitude claim is reversed, because a stiffer lung requires a larger, not smaller, intrapleural pressure change. (Choice B) is incorrect: it gives the right direction (larger pressure drop) but misstates the mechanism, because a stiff fibrotic lung has decreased, not increased, compliance and expands less readily, not more readily, per unit pressure. (Choice C) is incorrect: the pressure change needed depends on both the target volume and the lung's compliance, so at equal tidal volume the low-compliance lung still demands a larger intrapleural pressure change. This question tests the skill of reasoning from the compliance relationship (ΔV per ΔP) to predict how altered lung stiffness changes the intrapleural pressure required for a fixed tidal volume.$px$ WHERE id = '721bb77b-da9d-4ab0-94c9-80907d8c1063';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is C because quaternary structure is defined as the assembly of two or more polypeptide subunits, so a protein whose functional form is a single chain cannot have it. Quaternary structure is the overall arrangement that arises when separate protein chains associate with one another (either copies of the same chain or different proteins) to form a multi-subunit assembly; it is precisely this multi-subunit organization that enables cooperativity (e.g., hemoglobin's four subunits), whereas a single-chain protein such as myoglobin lacks it. Because the enzyme described is one folded chain, it has primary, secondary, and tertiary structure but stops short of quaternary. (Choice A) is incorrect because primary structure is simply the linear amino acid sequence, which every polypeptide possesses regardless of subunit number. (Choice B) is incorrect because tertiary structure is the overall three-dimensional folding of a single polypeptide chain, exactly what one folded chain has. (Choice C) is correct: with only one polypeptide there are no separate subunits to assemble, so no quaternary structure exists. (Choice D) is incorrect because secondary structure (the local backbone hydrogen-bonded \(\alpha\)-helices and \(\beta\)-sheets) forms within a single chain and is present here. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definition of each structural level to a described protein to deduce which one a single-chain protein cannot have.$px$ WHERE id = '7250ad7a-a995-47d7-ae22-d23bdfcdddd3';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Prokaryotes and Viruses (BIO)'. The answer is D because the organism is structurally prokaryotic (no nucleus, a single circular chromosome) yet carries the histone-like DNA-organizing proteins and the eukaryote-resembling transcription/translation machinery that are hallmarks of Archaea, not Bacteria. Domain assignment rests first on the nuclear criterion: "Microbes within the domains Bacteria and Archaea are all prokaryotes (their cells lack a nucleus), whereas microbes in the domain Eukarya are eukaryotes (their cells have a nucleus)." Because the cell has no nucleus, Eukarya is excluded outright regardless of how eukaryote-like its molecular parts are. Among the two prokaryotic domains, the deciding clue is that bacteria "lack histones" whereas archaea contain them, and "in archaea, the nucleoid is organized by either NAPs or histone-like DNA organizing proteins"; archaeal ribosomes and machinery are also "more similar to their eukaryotic counterparts than to those found in bacteria." Histones organize DNA in the cytoplasmic nucleoid of an archaeon just as they could in a nucleus, so their presence reflects archaeal ancestry, not a hidden eukaryotic identity. (Choice A) A single circular chromosome with no nucleus is shared by both Bacteria and Archaea, so it cannot select Bacteria; moreover, bacteria characteristically lack histones and use bacterial-type machinery, so the histone-like packaging and eukaryote-like polymerase actively point away from Bacteria. (Choice B) Histones are not exclusive to eukaryotes (archaea also possess histone-like proteins), and the absence of a nucleus is a defining, not developmental, feature, so this inference is the exact error the stem flags. (Choice C) This reverses the actual hierarchy of criteria: the presence or absence of a membrane-bound nucleus, not the resemblance of the transcription/translation machinery, is what separates the eukaryotic domain from the two prokaryotic domains; eukaryote-like machinery in a nucleus-free cell is precisely the archaeal signature. (Choice D) Correct: the absent nucleus and single circular chromosome make the cell prokaryotic, so Eukarya is excluded, while the histone-like DNA-organizing proteins and eukaryote-resembling transcription and translation machinery are the hallmarks that distinguish Archaea from Bacteria; because histones can organize DNA in a cytoplasmic nucleoid, their presence reflects archaeal ancestry rather than a eukaryotic identity. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must integrate several structural and molecular clues, apply the nuclear criterion before the molecular one, and reject a plausible but partial-truth inference to reach the correct domain.$px$ WHERE id = '730e8cb1-6f33-4dc6-8d15-c6562003a63a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Stoichiometry (GC)'. The answer to this question is C because the only reliable test for redox versus non-redox is to assign oxidation numbers to every element in all three steps and find the single step in which nothing changes. In step 1, elemental \(\text{S}\) and \(\text{O}_2\) each enter at \(0\); in \(\text{SO}_2\) oxygen is \(-2\) (so \(0 + S + 2(-2) = 0\) gives \(S = +4\)), so sulfur goes \(0 \rightarrow +4\) and oxygen goes \(0 \rightarrow -2\): step 1 is redox. In step 2, sulfur in \(\text{SO}_2\) is \(+4\) and in \(\text{SO}_3\) is \(+6\) (since \(S + 3(-2) = 0\)), while the added \(\text{O}_2\) goes from \(0\) to \(-2\); sulfur is oxidized \(+4 \rightarrow +6\) and oxygen is reduced \(0 \rightarrow -2\): step 2 is also redox. In step 3, sulfur is \(+6\) in \(\text{SO}_3\) and remains \(+6\) in \(\text{H}_2\text{SO}_4\) (for the neutral acid \(2(+1) + S + 4(-2) = 0\) gives \(S = +6\)); oxygen stays \(-2\) and hydrogen stays \(+1\) throughout. With no oxidation number changing, no electrons are transferred, so step 3 is the lone non-redox step: it is the hydration of an acid anhydride (a Lewis acid-base addition), not an oxidation-reduction. (Choice A) Being in standard states does not exempt a reaction from redox; in step 1 the free elements \(\text{S}\) and \(\text{O}_2\) start at \(0\) and end at \(+4\) and \(-2\) in \(\text{SO}_2\), so step 1 is in fact a redox combination, not the non-redox step. (Choice B) The mere reappearance of the same elements does not freeze their oxidation numbers; in step 2 sulfur rises from \(+4\) to \(+6\) and the newly added \(\text{O}_2\) falls from \(0\) to \(-2\), so step 2 is redox. (Choice D) This identifies the correct step but for the wrong reason: it confuses proton transfer (a Brønsted acid-base concept) with electron transfer (redox); a falling pH signals \(\text{H}^+\) release and is irrelevant to oxidation numbers, and proton exchange neither defines nor excludes redox. The absence of any oxidation-number change is what makes step 3 non-redox. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must assign and compare oxidation numbers across three chained transformations and isolate the single step in which none of them change.$px$ WHERE id = '738d3912-3c12-4e29-8b12-d8898d407f66';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is D because a protein that refolds correctly after complete denaturation (without a ribosome, chaperone, or any pre-existing higher-order structure) must carry all of its folding information within the only feature that survived: its linear amino acid sequence. A protein's primary structure dictates its ultimate three-dimensional structure and function. This is the basis of Anfinsen's dogma: the native 3D structure of such proteins is uniquely encoded in the primary structure (the amino acid sequence) and is kinetically accessible, so that refolding can be rapid and efficient for small proteins. In short, the sequence determines the structure, which, in turn, determines the function of the protein. (Choice A) The ribosome catalyzes peptide bond formation during synthesis, but the experiment shows refolding occurs with no ribosome present, so the translation machinery cannot be what specifies the fold. (Choice B) is a reversed relationship: \(\alpha\)-helices are secondary structures that arise from the sequence as regular repeating structures formed by backbone hydrogen bonds, so they are a product of folding, not a pre-existing template that directs it, and all such structure was destroyed by denaturation. (Choice C) is a partial truth: disulfide bonds do stabilize both tertiary and quaternary structures, but they were reduced and then re-formed correctly only because the cysteine positions in the sequence guided refolding, so the bonds reflect the fold rather than specify it. (Choice D) is correct: the only feature retained through denaturation and renaturation is the covalent order of residues, and that linear sequence is the encoded determinant of the native fold. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply Anfinsen's principle to an experimental refolding outcome and reason out which surviving feature can account for it.$px$ WHERE id = '73d33a6c-f7cf-4393-b0ad-49440201dadf';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is C because starch is a polymer of glucose, so its breakdown by amylase yields maltose, a disaccharide composed of two glucose units joined by an \(\alpha\)-1,4 glycosidic bond. The intestinal brush-border enzyme maltase then hydrolyzes maltose into two free glucose molecules for absorption.

(Choice C) is correct: Maltose is the disaccharide intermediate of starch digestion, and because starch contains only glucose residues, both monosaccharides released are glucose.

(Choice B) is incorrect: One glucose plus one fructose describes sucrose, \(\text{Glc}(\alpha 1,2)\text{Fru}\), which is table sugar derived from plants, not the intermediate produced when amylase digests starch.

(Choice A) is incorrect: One galactose plus one glucose describes lactose, \(\text{Gal}(\beta 1,4)\text{Glc}\), the disaccharide found in milk; galactose is not a component of starch, so it cannot arise from starch breakdown.

(Choice D) is incorrect: Two fructose units would require a fructose-based polymer; starch is built entirely from glucose, so no fructose is released during its digestion.

This question requires Skill 1 (Knowledge of Scientific Concepts) because it tests recall of the composition and dietary origin of maltose as the disaccharide product of starch hydrolysis.$px$ WHERE id = '742dd210-17c8-42e4-8b28-b1de1e7705be';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is D because after a carbohydrate meal the hepatocyte's job is to convert incoming glucose into stored glycogen, and insulin's four enzyme changes all push flux in that single direction. Activated glucokinase phosphorylates glucose to glucose-6-phosphate, trapping it inside the cell; activated glycogen synthase incorporates that glucose-6-phosphate (carried by UDP-glucose) into glycogen; inhibited glycogen phosphorylase can no longer break glycogen back down; and inhibited glucose-6-phosphatase can no longer regenerate free glucose for export. The combined result is net glycogen synthesis with glucose held inside the liver.

(Choice A) is incorrect: this describes the fasting, glucagon-driven pattern of glycogen breakdown plus dephosphorylation of glucose-6-phosphate so free glucose can leave the cell. Insulin, released when blood glucose is high, runs the opposite program of storage, not mobilization.

(Choice B) is incorrect: glucokinase is activated, not held inactive, and free glucose cannot polymerize into glycogen directly. Glucose must first be phosphorylated to glucose-6-phosphate before glycogen synthase can add it, and that phosphorylation by glucokinase is exactly what traps the glucose in the cell.

(Choice D) is correct: glucokinase traps glucose as glucose-6-phosphate, glycogen synthase channels it into glycogen, inhibited phosphorylase halts breakdown, and inhibited glucose-6-phosphatase blocks glucose export. Those four changes converge on net glycogen storage.

(Choice C) is incorrect: activating glycogen synthase alone would be undercut if phosphorylase kept degrading glycogen and glucose-6-phosphatase kept exporting glucose. The net direction is set by all four coordinated changes acting together, not by glycogen synthase in isolation.

This question rewards Skill 2 (reasoning): you had to trace how several coordinated enzyme changes combine to determine the net direction of hepatic glycogen metabolism rather than recall a single fact.$px$, options = $px$[{"label":"A","text":"This is the enzyme pattern glucagon imposes during a fast: the liver breaks glycogen down and dephosphorylates glucose-6-phosphate so free glucose can be exported, raising blood glucose."},{"label":"B","text":"Free glucose entering the hepatocyte polymerizes directly into glycogen; because glucokinase is held inactive, the glucose is never phosphorylated and therefore cannot leak back out, which is what favors storage."},{"label":"C","text":"Net glycogen storage results solely from activating glycogen synthase; the changes to glucokinase, phosphorylase, and glucose-6-phosphatase are incidental and do not affect the direction of glycogen metabolism."},{"label":"D","text":"Glucokinase traps incoming glucose as glucose-6-phosphate and glycogen synthase channels it into glycogen, while inhibited glycogen phosphorylase halts breakdown and inhibited glucose-6-phosphatase blocks glucose export, so net flux runs toward glycogen storage."}]$px$::jsonb WHERE id = '7468bd14-cdaf-47d7-bc9b-830e30cfc310';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Oxidative Phosphorylation.'

The answer is B because respiration rate depends on whether the proton gradient can discharge. Oligomycin blocks ATP synthase, so protons have nowhere to return; the gradient builds up and its backpressure opposes further pumping, slowing electron transport and lowering \(\text{O}_2\) consumption. An uncoupler provides an alternate return path, keeping the gradient low and removing backpressure, so the pumps run freely and \(\text{O}_2\) consumption rises. The two agents therefore move respiration in opposite directions.

(Choice B) is correct: it correctly links the undischarged gradient (backpressure) to reduced \(\text{O}_2\) use with oligomycin and the dissipated gradient (no backpressure) to increased \(\text{O}_2\) use with the uncoupler.

(Choice A) is incorrect: only oligomycin lowers \(\text{O}_2\) consumption; the uncoupler does not act through ATP synthase and actually raises respiration by dissipating the gradient.

(Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses both effects. Oligomycin slows rather than speeds respiration (the gradient cannot discharge), and the uncoupler speeds rather than slows it.

(Choice D) is incorrect: oligomycin does not increase \(\text{O}_2\) consumption; its buildup of the gradient creates backpressure that decreases it, so the two agents are not equivalent.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, the student must reason from gradient backpressure to opposite respiratory outcomes rather than recall a fact.$px$ WHERE id = '753af259-0d8c-46d6-bedd-b29334731c02';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is D because quiet (resting) exhalation is a passive process powered by the elastic recoil of the stretched lung tissue; when that elastic tissue is destroyed, the lung no longer springs back forcefully, so air is not expelled efficiently and becomes trapped, retained in the lungs after each breath. (Choice D) is correct: Loss of recoil removes the passive driving force for resting exhalation, so exhalation is impaired and air is retained. That retention is the defining consequence of diminished elastic recoil. (Choice A) is incorrect: Inhalation is an active process driven by contraction of the diaphragm and external intercostals, which enlarge the thorax; it does not depend on elastic recoil, so resting inhalation is not the step most directly impaired (in fact, an over-compliant lung is easier, not harder, to inflate). (Choice B) is incorrect: It correctly notes that internal intercostals and abdominal muscles can drive exhalation, but those muscles power only forced exhalation; quiet exhalation at rest is passive and recoil-driven, so losing recoil does impair resting exhalation. (Choice C) is incorrect: Recoil does not drive inhalation (inspiration is muscle-driven), so the deficit is not symmetric; only the passive, recoil-dependent exhalation phase is directly compromised. This item assesses the skill of predicting a physiological consequence from an altered structural property (loss of elastic recoil).$px$ WHERE id = '754b3011-025e-40f4-b1ca-7d2971ca869b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).' The three cytoskeletal fibers differ in both diameter and function: microfilaments (~7 nm) are intertwined actin strands that drive movement and contractility, intermediate filaments (~8 to 10 nm) are wound fibrous-protein strands that bear tension, and microtubules (~25 nm) are hollow tubes of \(\alpha\)-tubulin/\(\beta\)-tubulin dimers that resist compression and serve as tracks along which vesicles move through the cell. Two clues must agree: the ~25 nm diameter identifies the widest fiber, and directed vesicle transport is the job assigned specifically to that fiber. (Choice A) is correct: only microtubules are ~25 nm (the widest fiber) and act as the tracks for directed vesicle transport, and they are composed of polymerized \(\alpha\)-tubulin/\(\beta\)-tubulin dimers. Both the size and the transport role match. (Choice B) is incorrect: microfilaments are the narrowest fibers (~7 nm) of intertwined actin and function in cellular movement and contractility, not as the ~25 nm transport highways described, so the diameter rules them out. (Choice C) is incorrect: intermediate filaments (~8 to 10 nm) are wound fibrous-protein strands whose role is purely structural (bearing tension and anchoring organelles), not carrying vesicles, and they are far narrower than 25 nm. (Choice D) is incorrect: it correctly labels the fiber a microtubule but assigns it the actin composition of a microfilament; microtubules are made of tubulin dimers, not intertwined actin strands. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the measured diameter and the observed transport behavior together to identify the fiber and infer its correct composition, rather than recalling a single fact.$px$ WHERE id = '754cd5b2-10c7-4619-9ae2-8e12ec611209';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Reproductive Endocrinology (BIO)'. The answer is C because hCG from the implanting blastocyst's chorion keeps the corpus luteum active, and the resulting sustained progesterone is what maintains the endometrium so it is not shed. Normally, "the corpus luteum degenerates and the levels of estrogen and progesterone decrease" and "the endometrium begins to degenerate as the progesterone levels drop," which initiates menstruation. When implantation occurs, the chorion "begins releasing a hormone called human beta chorionic gonadotropin (β-HCG) which makes its way to the corpus luteum and keeps that structure active. This ensures adequate levels of progesterone that will maintain the endometrium." Thus the rescue chain runs blastocyst chorion to hCG to corpus luteum to progesterone to maintained endometrium, exactly the sequence the researcher observes. (Choice A) is wrong because shedding is triggered by the drop in progesterone from the degenerating corpus luteum, not by falling estrogen, and the corpus luteum does not persist on its own. It requires the hCG signal. (Choice B) is wrong because hCG does not act directly on the uterine lining; it acts on the corpus luteum to sustain progesterone, which in turn maintains the lining. (Choice C) is correct because hCG keeps the corpus luteum active, sustaining the high progesterone that preserves the endometrium. (Choice D) is wrong because hCG itself substitutes for LH's support of the corpus luteum rather than working by driving the anterior pituitary to release FSH and LH. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the endocrine fate of the endometrium by tracing a multi-step hormonal feedback chain from an implantation scenario to the maintained-lining outcome.$px$ WHERE id = '7577f852-8e11-4809-8e8a-66b1e91df018';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is B because, at temperatures below an enzyme's optimum, a moderate increase in temperature speeds up the catalyzed reaction. From about 0 to 40-50 °C, enzyme activity generally increases, as do the rates of most uncatalyzed reactions, and as a rule of thumb reaction velocities roughly double for each 10 °C rise. Moderate increases in temperature typically enhance reaction rates, whereas excessive heat leads to irreversible denaturation. Because the warming described keeps the enzyme below its optimum (short of denaturation), the expected effect is a faster reaction rate. (Choice A) is incorrect: the activation energy is lowered by the enzyme itself, not by added heat; enzymes lower the activation energy for bound transition states and change the reaction mechanism, whereas raising the temperature simply gives molecules more energy to surmount the existing barrier rather than lowering it. (Choice B) is correct: below the optimum, a moderate temperature increase raises the reaction rate, consistent with enzyme activity increasing and reaction velocities roughly doubling per 10 °C. (Choice C) is incorrect: temperature changes within this range do not raise the equilibrium constant through the enzyme, because enzymes do not affect the thermodynamics of reactions. For a reversible reaction, the equilibrium constant, Keq, is unchanged; a catalyst alters how fast equilibrium is reached, not \(K_{eq}\). (Choice D) is incorrect: \(K_m\) is the substrate concentration at which the initial velocity is half of Vmax, a binding/saturation parameter, and temperature effects here produce increased activity followed eventually by denaturation, not a decrease in \(K_m\). This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the temperature-dependence of enzyme activity below the optimum and the principle that catalysts change reaction rates but not thermodynamic constants to predict which single quantity changes when the assay is modestly warmed.$px$ WHERE id = '7582c9d7-4ba0-4d63-ace3-65991d02c912';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions.' A molecule is polar only if it contains polar bonds AND its molecular geometry is such that the individual bond dipole vectors do not cancel; each bond dipole points from the less electronegative atom toward the more electronegative one, and the net molecular dipole is their vector sum in three dimensions. When identical polar bonds are arranged symmetrically around a central atom, their vectors cancel and the molecule is nonpolar despite having polar bonds. (Choice D) is correct: \(\text{NH}_3\) is trigonal pyramidal because the central nitrogen carries a lone pair, so the three polar N-H bond dipoles do not cancel and add to a nonzero net dipole moment, making ammonia polar. (Choice A) is incorrect: \(\text{CO}_2\) is linear, so its two equal but oppositely directed C=O bond dipoles point \(180^\circ\) apart and cancel exactly, leaving a nonpolar molecule despite the polar bonds. (Choice B) is incorrect: \(\text{BF}_3\) is trigonal planar with three identical B-F bond dipoles separated by \(120^\circ\); by symmetry their vector sum is zero, so the molecule is nonpolar. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{CCl}_4\) is tetrahedral with four identical C-Cl bond dipoles arranged symmetrically, so they cancel and the molecule has no net dipole moment even though each bond is polar. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply VSEPR-derived molecular geometry to the vector sum of bond dipoles to decide which molecule has a net dipole, rather than judging bond polarity alone.$px$ WHERE id = '75f5137e-fcc8-46a8-9e0a-fd8881753cd4';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' Side chains are sorted into nonpolar, polar uncharged, acidic, or basic classes not by which atoms they contain but by the property those atoms actually confer: the polar uncharged class is defined by heteroatoms (O, S, or N) that form a permanent dipole within the R-group, whereas a side chain whose heteroatom does not create such a dipole stays nonpolar. The two sulfur residues sit on opposite sides of this line: cysteine's free sulfhydryl (\(-\text{SH}\)) forms a permanent dipole, but methionine's terminal thioether does not generate a comparable dipole within the R-group and stays poorly soluble, so methionine is classified as a nonpolar, hydrophobic amino acid. (Choice A) is incorrect: it assumes that merely containing a sulfur heteroatom makes a side chain hydrophilic, but classification depends on whether that heteroatom forms a permanent dipole, which methionine's thioether does not. (Choice B) is correct: cysteine's sulfhydryl forms a permanent dipole and places it in the polar uncharged class alongside serine and threonine, while methionine's dipole-free thioether keeps it nonpolar. (Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses the two assignments, and no greater electronegativity of the thioether drives polarity. It is the sulfhydryl of cysteine, not the thioether of methionine, that produces the dipole. (Choice D) is incorrect: it overgeneralizes methionine's nonpolar behavior to cysteine, ignoring that the free \(-\text{SH}\) of cysteine does form a permanent dipole and makes cysteine polar uncharged. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the permanent-dipole criterion for the polar uncharged class to two chemically similar residues and infer that identical elemental composition does not guarantee identical classification.$px$ WHERE id = '76d5de93-0e32-4c0b-a1ad-a71245eaa6ba';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'The Action Potential'. The answer is D because at threshold two forces act on Na+ in the same direction: its chemical concentration gradient and the electrical gradient. The text states that "the concentration of Na+ is higher outside the cell than inside the cell by a factor of 10, ions will rush into the cell that are driven largely by the concentration gradient," and adds that "the electrical gradient also plays a role, as negative proteins below the membrane attract the sodium ion." Because the interior sits near -55 mV (still negative) when the channels open, the cell's negative charge attracts the positively charged cation inward even as the 10-fold outside-to-inside concentration difference pushes it in, so both forces reinforce each other. (Choice A) is wrong because the Na+/K+ pump is an ATP-driven active transporter that moves Na+ outward against its gradient to maintain that gradient; it does not pump ions through passive voltage-gated channels. (Choice B) is a partial truth: the concentration gradient does push Na+ in, but the membrane voltage does not oppose entry the whole time. While the interior is negative it actively attracts Na+, so the electrical force aids entry rather than opposing it. (Choice C) reverses the concentration relationship: Na+ is higher outside the cell, not inside, so it is not driven by the electrical gradient alone. (Choice D) is correct because both the concentration gradient and the electrical attraction of the negative interior drive Na+ inward. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires applying the dual electrochemical driving forces to a specific voltage condition (-55 mV) to predict the direction and cause of Na+ flux rather than recalling a single fact.$px$ WHERE id = '77276126-f958-4383-964e-71c3e381becf';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Early Embryonic Development.' The egg mounts two defenses against polyspermy that differ in speed and mechanism: a fast block, in which the egg's membrane potential changes almost instantaneously the moment the first sperm binds (Ernest Everett Just's "wave of negativity"), electrically preventing further fusion; and a slow block, in which the egg alters its membrane structure by discharging cortical granules, building a fertilization membrane over roughly the next half-minute. Because the fast block is purely electrical and the slow block is powered by granule exocytosis, depleting the granules while leaving the membrane's electrical machinery (its ion channels) intact should knock out only the slow, structural defense. (Choice A) is incorrect: hardening the zona into a fertilization membrane is the granule-dependent slow block, so depleting cortical granules abolishes it. It cannot be the defense that survived. (Choice B) is correct: the only defense that operates within the first second or two is the fast block, and because it is an electrical change in membrane potential rather than a product of granule release, it remains fully functional after granule depletion. That exactly matches the observed brief early protection followed by later polyspermy once the (now-absent) slow block should have taken over. (Choice C) is incorrect: the mucopolysaccharide coating that forms the fertilization membrane is deposited by the cortical granules themselves, so it is precisely what fails when granules are depleted, and it is a slow (~30 s) process, not a first-second one. (Choice D) is incorrect: the acrosomal reaction is a sperm event that discharges enzymes to breach the zona so fusion can occur. It promotes fertilization rather than blocking polyspermy, so it is not an egg defense at all. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the distinction between the electrical fast block and the granule-driven slow block to predict, from a manipulation that removes granules while sparing the membrane's ion channels, which defense remains and why.$px$ WHERE id = '774ad094-5762-445d-8648-09cbebf96132';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).' In the secretory pathway, a protein is synthesized and folded in the rough endoplasmic reticulum (RER), then travels through the Golgi apparatus (the stack of flattened sacs described in the stem) before being packaged for export; the direction of flow is fixed, entering at the Golgi's cis face and leaving at its trans face. Transport vesicles that form from the ER travel to the cis face, fuse with it, and release their contents into the Golgi lumen, and the vesicles carrying the integral protein bud from the ER and fuse with the Golgi's cis face, so the vesicle arriving at the cis face comes from the ER, not the Golgi. (Choice A) is correct: the RER is where the antibody was synthesized, folded, and given its first sugar chains; a protein that is not destined to remain in the RER leaves via transport vesicles that bud from the RER's membrane, and these are the ER-derived vesicles that fuse with the Golgi's cis face. (Choice B) is incorrect: the Golgi apparatus is the stack of flattened sacs described in the stem itself, so it cannot be the source of the vesicle arriving at its own cis face; vesicles leave the Golgi from the opposite (trans) face as secretory vesicles that bud from the Golgi's trans face. (Choice C) is incorrect: the smooth ER carries out synthesis of carbohydrates, lipids, and steroid hormones; detoxification of medications and poisons; and storage of calcium ions; it lacks ribosomes and does not synthesize the secreted antibody or feed protein-laden vesicles to the Golgi cis face. (Choice D) is incorrect: the nuclear envelope is a double-membrane structure whose pores control the passage of ions, molecules, and RNA between the nucleus and cytoplasm; although continuous with the ER, it is not the organelle that buds the antibody-carrying transport vesicles that fuse with the Golgi. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply your knowledge of the direction of flow through the endomembrane system (recognizing that the cis face receives vesicles arriving from the RER) rather than simply matching the description of sorting and packaging to the Golgi.$px$ WHERE id = '77578e74-81a0-4e86-a4f4-f2b2b4f31745';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is B because feedback inhibition is a regulatory mechanism in which a downstream end product binds an allosteric site on an early enzyme to switch its own production off once enough has been made. For example, serine, the end product of its pathway, binds an allosteric site on PGDH and inhibits it (a classic example of feedback inhibition of the first reaction of a pathway by the end product), and CTP and UTP feed back to allosterically inhibit ATCase, preventing a buildup of pyrimidine nucleotides. Because allosteric regulation lets the cell adjust enzyme activity according to surrounding environmental conditions through feedback control loops driven by downstream products, the pathway is dialed down when product is plentiful, conserving resources and maintaining internal homeostasis. (Choice A) is wrong because feedback inhibition does the opposite of maximizing output: rising end product inhibits the enzyme rather than driving faster synthesis. (Choice B) is correct: it prevents overaccumulation of end product and maintains homeostasis by shutting the pathway off when product is sufficient, exactly as CTP/UTP feedback prevents a buildup of nucleotides. (Choice C) is wrong because feedback inhibition acts by reversible allosteric binding that produces a conformational change, not by permanent denaturation; the enzyme is transiently inhibited, not destroyed. (Choice D) is wrong because feedback inhibition slows the pathway at the first committed step rather than accelerating every step; accelerating all steps would describe activation, not inhibition. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the concept of end-product feedback inhibition to infer its physiological purpose, reasoning from why a cell would allosterically shut down its own biosynthetic pathway once product is abundant rather than simply recalling a definition.$px$ WHERE id = '776eeb18-04af-46c4-802c-cee1d510ff2a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is A because an enzyme is a biological catalyst that speeds a reaction only by lowering the activation energy (\(E_a\)) needed to reach the transition state; it does not alter the reactants' or products' free energy, so the reaction's \(\Delta G\) is unchanged. Because enzymes do not change \(\Delta G\), they do not change whether a reaction is exergonic (spontaneous) or endergonic; and because they leave the thermodynamics of the reaction untouched, the equilibrium constant \(K_\text{eq}\) is unchanged as well. What changes is only the rate at which equilibrium is reached. An endergonic reaction therefore remains endergonic with the enzyme present; the enzyme only makes it reach equilibrium faster. (Choice A) is correct: \(\Delta G\) depends solely on the free energy of reactants and products, which the enzyme does not touch. (Choice B) is wrong because the enzyme does not lower \(\Delta G\) or flip its sign; a positive \(\Delta G\) stays positive. (Choice C) is wrong because the enzyme increases only the rate at which equilibrium is reached, not the equilibrium position; the balance of reactants and products is unchanged. (Choice D) is wrong because \(K_\text{eq}\) reflects the equilibrium position, which the enzyme leaves unchanged. It only increases how fast equilibrium is reached, not where it lies. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that catalysis affects kinetics but not thermodynamics to a specific endergonic reaction.$px$ WHERE id = '77b6cbe9-3990-46a1-bb86-f5c0549ad953';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Nervous System Organization.' The peripheral nervous system's functional divisions are distinguished chiefly by the effector tissue each one commands: the somatic nervous system innervates skeletal muscle, while the autonomic nervous system innervates smooth muscle, cardiac muscle, and glands. A voluntary motor response is defined as the contraction of skeletal muscle, but those contractions are not always voluntary: being startled and jumping back is a reflex involving skeletal muscle contractions. That is a somatic response that occurs without a conscious decision. (Choice C) is correct: jumping back and screaming are both produced by contractions of skeletal muscle (limb, trunk, and laryngeal muscles), and skeletal muscle is the effector the somatic division commands, so the somatic system carried this motor signal even though the movement was reflexive. (Choice A) is incorrect: lack of conscious control does not by itself make a response autonomic. Reflexes and habit-learned movements are involuntary yet remain somatic because they act on skeletal muscle; the division is defined by the effector tissue, not by whether a decision was made. (Choice B) is incorrect: although a startle does trigger a sympathetic surge, that autonomic output drives smooth/cardiac muscle and glands (racing heart, sweating), not the skeletal-muscle contractions that constitute the actual jumping and screaming. (Choice D) is incorrect: the enteric nervous system controls the smooth muscle and glandular tissue of the digestive tract, not the skeletal muscles that move the trunk and limbs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the effector-tissue basis of the somatic-versus-autonomic distinction to a reflexive skeletal-muscle response and resist the trap that "involuntary" equals autonomic.$px$ WHERE id = '78094775-f665-45dc-848f-2af5d02bcd3c';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is C because X inactivation is random in each early embryonic cell but is then clonally inherited, so a contiguous patch of skin descends from a single progenitor cell whose descendants all share the same silenced X and therefore express the same coat color allele. In female mammalian embryos, one X chromosome in each cell inactivates by condensing into a Barr body; which X (maternally or paternally derived) is silenced in a given cell is random, but once that choice is made, all cells derived from it will carry the same inactive X chromosome, or Barr body. Because a heterozygote will express one of two different coat colors over different regions of the body (corresponding to whichever X chromosome inactivated in that region's embryonic cell progenitor), each region is a clone, producing discrete patches rather than a blended phenotype. (Choice A) Incomplete dominance would yield a single uniform intermediate phenotype across the whole animal, which is the opposite of the discrete-patch pattern; the variegation arises from X inactivation, not from an allelic dominance interaction. (Choice B) Independent assortment describes how alleles of different genes distribute into gametes and operates before fertilization, so it cannot explain a spatial mosaic arising within the body of a single individual during embryonic development. (Choice C) is correct: random silencing fixed by clonal descent means every cell in a contiguous region carries the same inactive X and expresses the same allele, generating sharp-edged color patches rather than a uniform blend. (Choice D) Only one X is inactivated per cell, not both; a female inactivates all but one X chromosome in each of her cells, so at least one X always stays active to provide essential gene products. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principles of random X inactivation and clonal inheritance to explain an observed spatial coat-color pattern.$px$ WHERE id = '7830bef8-bef1-4059-b06d-d49ce8a8e8fe';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Biosignaling.' The answer is A because, for a receptor tyrosine kinase, ligand binding acts by driving two monomers together into a dimer, and it is this dimerization that activates the cytoplasmic kinase domain (leading to autophosphorylation of the partner using ATP); artificially clamping two monomers together therefore switches on the intracellular catalytic activity even without ligand. (Choice A) is correct: the activating event is monomer dimerization, so any means of forcing two monomers together (including a crosslinker in ligand-free medium) reproduces the activation the ligand normally causes. (Choice B) is incorrect: it reflects the misconception that continuous occupancy of the extracellular site is what directly turns on catalysis, whereas the mechanistic role of ligand binding is to promote dimerization, which the crosslinker supplies independently. (Choice C) is incorrect: it reflects the misconception that the extracellular signal molecule is itself the enzyme's phosphoacceptor substrate; the kinase phosphorylates tyrosine residues on the partner receptor (autophosphorylation), and the crosslinker is not imported or phosphorylated. (Choice D) is incorrect: it confuses the receptor-enzyme mechanism with the G-protein-coupled receptor pathway, in which a G protein and downstream enzyme generate a diffusible second messenger; a receptor tyrosine kinase has intrinsic catalytic activity and does not act through an associated G protein. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that a catalytic (enzyme-linked) receptor's intracellular enzymatic activity is triggered by ligand-induced dimerization rather than by mere occupancy of the binding site or by a G-protein-generated second messenger.$px$ WHERE id = '783cf026-f78a-45a1-ad58-9734b9e596f2';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is C because the stem supplies every fact needed to trace the block. A long-chain fatty acyl-CoA cannot cross the inner mitochondrial membrane on its own; \(\text{CPT-I}\) on the outer membrane must first transfer the acyl group from \(\text{CoA}\) onto carnitine, the acyl-carnitine is then ferried across, and \(\text{CPT-II}\) inside regenerates the acyl-CoA. \(\text{CPT-I}\) is the rate-limiting, committed step of fatty-acid oxidation, and the stem states that malonyl-CoA is high and inhibits \(\text{CPT-I}\). With that gatekeeper shut down, the acyl group is never loaded onto carnitine, so the acyl-CoA is stranded on the cytosolic side and matrix beta-oxidation is suppressed. This is the reciprocal-regulation checkpoint: while the cell is building fat (high malonyl-CoA from active acetyl-CoA carboxylase), it should not simultaneously burn it.

(Choice C) is correct: inhibiting \(\text{CPT-I}\), the rate-limiting shuttle enzyme, prevents formation of acyl-carnitine, so the acyl group cannot enter the matrix and beta-oxidation is turned off. That is exactly the outcome expected when malonyl-CoA is high.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it misassigns the malonyl-CoA-sensitive, rate-limiting role to \(\text{CPT-II}\) and assumes long-chain acyl-CoA can cross the inner membrane on its own. Malonyl-CoA inhibits \(\text{CPT-I}\), not \(\text{CPT-II}\), and long-chain acyl-CoA is membrane-impermeant without the carnitine shuttle.
(Choice B) is incorrect: it reverses the direction of the effect. Blocking the committed entry step cannot accelerate downstream oxidation. With no substrate delivered to the matrix, the beta-oxidation enzymes have nothing to act on, so flux falls rather than rises.
(Choice D) is incorrect: it treats carnitine as a passive carrier that moves acyl groups without \(\text{CPT-I}\). Carnitine only accepts the acyl group because \(\text{CPT-I}\) catalyzes the transesterification; with \(\text{CPT-I}\) inhibited there is no acyl-carnitine to shuttle, so oxidation does not continue unchanged.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, this question asks you to chain localization, the rate-limiting identity of \(\text{CPT-I}\), and allosteric regulation by malonyl-CoA into a single predicted consequence rather than to recall a definition.$px$ WHERE id = '788411a5-f55e-49ce-b4a6-cd68b2b51496';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question in the content category 'The Periodic Table: Classification of Elements Into Groups by Electronic Structure'. Bonding behavior follows directly from the metal/nonmetal classification of the two combining elements: a metal paired with a nonmetal transfers electrons to form a cation-anion (ionic) compound, whereas two nonmetals have comparable, high electronegativities and neither can fully strip electrons from the other, so they share valence electrons in covalent bonds. Both \(\text{N}\) and \(\text{O}\) are nonmetals occupying the upper-right p-block, so the compound they form is held together by covalent (molecular) bonding, as in \(\text{NO}\) or \(\text{NO}_2\), making C correct. (Choice A) Complete electron transfer to give discrete ions such as \(\text{N}^{3+}\) and \(\text{O}^{2-}\) characterizes a metal-nonmetal pairing; two nonmetals of similar electronegativity do not produce a full transfer, so an ionic description does not apply here. (Choice B) Metallic bonding, a delocalized electron sea, requires metal atoms with loosely held valence electrons; nonmetals hold their valence electrons tightly and do not delocalize them across a lattice. (Choice D) Relative horizontal position within the same nonmetal region does not convert the left-hand element into a metal; \(\text{N}\) remains a nonmetal regardless of its position relative to \(\text{O}\), so it does not act as an electron-donating metal. Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = '78edbca2-2100-41c4-8998-29ebcf0b12ce';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Endocrine System: Hormones and Their Sources.' The answer is B because the hormone described is thyroid hormone (T3/T4), which binds intracellular receptors on mitochondria and initiates transcription of genes for nutrient breakdown and glucose oxidation, raising the body's basal metabolic rate. When present in excess, it increases nutrient breakdown and the use of oxygen to produce ATP; because this ATP production is inefficient, abnormally increased heat is released as a byproduct (the calorigenic effect), raising body temperature. Thus elevated levels drive up oxygen consumption and heat output together. (Choice B) is correct: elevated thyroid hormone increases cellular respiration, so oxygen use climbs, and the inefficient ATP synthesis releases surplus heat, elevating metabolic rate and body temperature. (Choice A) is incorrect: blood calcium is lowered by calcitonin (from the thyroid's parafollicular cells) and raised by parathyroid hormone; calcium homeostasis is a separate hormonal axis from the metabolic-rate hormone described, and elevated metabolic hormone does not itself drive calcium down. (Choice C) is incorrect: fasting blood glucose is lowered chiefly by insulin; the metabolic hormone described actually promotes glucose oxidation and, in excess, tends to accompany catabolism rather than a hypoglycemic set point, so predicting lowered glucose misattributes insulin's role. (Choice D) is incorrect: renal sodium handling is governed largely by aldosterone (and, for water, ADH); the calorigenic metabolic hormone does not act primarily on sodium reabsorption, so this confuses the mineralocorticoid axis with the metabolic one. This question requires Skill 2 reasoning because the student must map a described mechanism onto its downstream physiological effect (predicting increased oxygen use and heat from an elevated metabolic hormone) rather than recalling a stated definition.$px$ WHERE id = '790fdbef-e945-4008-9dec-5a7e99f959c1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Gastrulation & Germ Layers.' During gastrulation the three primary germ layers are established, and each is programmed to give rise to a specific set of adult tissues: the ectoderm forms the outer epidermis and the central nervous system, the endoderm forms the epithelial lining of the digestive and respiratory tracts, and the mesoderm forms muscle, connective tissue, blood, and most visceral organs. To identify the labeled layer you must classify each observed tissue by its embryonic origin. Recognize, for example, that the dermis is connective tissue (mesoderm) even though the epidermis directly above it is ectoderm, and that a blood-vessel wall is mesodermal even though the space it encloses connects to organs of other origins. (Choice C) is correct: the mesoderm gives rise to all specialized muscle tissues, connective tissues such as the skeleton and blood cells, and most visceral organs such as the kidneys and the spleen; the dermis is dermal connective tissue, the vessel walls and heart are muscular/connective circulatory structures, and the kidneys are the excretory visceral organs, so all four labeled tissues trace to mesoderm while the unlabeled epidermis, lung lining, and brain do not. (Choice A) is incorrect: the endoderm does form the epithelial lining of the lungs and gut, but those tissues were explicitly unlabeled in this experiment, so a layer whose derivatives are unlabeled cannot be the marked layer. (Choice B) is incorrect: the ectoderm does form the epidermis and the brain, but both of those were unlabeled as well, so ectoderm is contradicted by the same evidence that its listed derivatives carry no label. (Choice D) is incorrect: although the kidneys and blood-vessel walls were indeed labeled, these are mesodermal derivatives, not endodermal ones. The endoderm produces the lining of the gut and respiratory tract, so assigning excretory and circulatory tissues to the endoderm misidentifies their germ-layer origin. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the known derivatives of each germ layer to an observed labeling pattern in order to deduce which layer was marked.$px$ WHERE id = '792b2844-3ab4-44bc-b542-9638fedfc2e5';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is A because only statement I is consistent with the pedigree. With X-linked recessive disorders, a male needs to inherit only one recessive mutant X allele to be affected, so these disorders are disproportionately observed in males, and unaffected female carriers can pass the trait to their sons, who then express it. A pedigree in which affected sons \(X^a Y\) are repeatedly born to unaffected parents, are linked through unaffected \(X^A X^a\) carrier mothers, and in which no affected females appear is the signature of X-linked recessive transmission, making statement I correct and the answer "I only." (Choice A) is correct: statement I alone is supported, because the carrier-mother-to-affected-son pattern with no affected daughters is exactly the inheritance expected for X-linked recessive traits. (Choice B) is wrong because it requires statement III, which is false: because a male needs only one mutant X allele while a female must inherit recessive X-linked alleles from both parents to express the trait, the disorder appears more frequently in males than in females, so affected females are NOT as frequent as affected males. (Choice C) is wrong because, although it correctly includes statement I, it also includes the false statement III for the same reason. (Choice D) is wrong because it includes statements II and III, both false: for statement II, an affected father \(X^a Y\) passes his Y to every son and his \(X^a\) only to daughters, so his sons inherit his Y chromosome and receive the trait from the mother, not the father. A father transmits an X-linked recessive allele to his daughters, not his sons. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rules of X-linked recessive inheritance to a described pedigree to evaluate which conclusions the data support.$px$ WHERE id = '796ea85d-cc62-4994-a66c-83a3978dd7d4';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.'

The answer is A because the O₂ pressure at half-saturation is \(P_{50}\), and \(P_{50}\) is inversely related to oxygen affinity: a higher \(P_{50}\) means more O₂ pressure is required to half-saturate the protein, so the protein holds O₂ less tightly (lower affinity). Sample Y has the highest \(P_{50}\) (31 mm Hg), so it has the lowest O₂ affinity of the four samples. A low-affinity hemoglobin gives up its bound O₂ most readily as the surrounding O₂ pressure falls in a respiring tissue, so Sample Y unloads the greatest fraction of its oxygen to the tissue.

(Choice A) is correct: Y's \(P_{50}\) of 31 mm Hg is the largest value, marking it as the lowest-affinity sample; low affinity is exactly what maximizes O₂ release at low tissue \(pO_2\).

(Choice B) is incorrect: Sample Z does reach half-saturation at the lowest O₂ pressure (12 mm Hg), but a low \(P_{50}\) signals the highest affinity, not the lowest. High-affinity hemoglobin clings to O₂ and unloads the least in the tissues, so Z is the poorest, not the best, oxygen deliverer. This choice inverts the \(P_{50}\)-affinity relationship.

(Choice C) is incorrect: proximity of a \(P_{50}\) value to the middle of the data set has no bearing on oxygen delivery. The relevant comparison is which sample has the lowest affinity (highest \(P_{50}\)), not which value is near the median; W's mid-range \(P_{50}\) simply makes it an intermediate deliverer.

(Choice D) is incorrect: matching \(P_{50}\) to a particular tissue \(pO_2\) does not determine which sample unloads the most oxygen. Unloading is governed by how low the affinity is (how high the \(P_{50}\) is) relative to the others; a numerical coincidence between 26 mm Hg and a resting-tissue pressure is a distractor, and Y still delivers a greater fraction than X.

To reason from the data alone, treat the half-saturation pressure as \(P_{50}\), rank the four values, recognize that the largest \(P_{50}\) corresponds to the weakest O₂ binding, and connect weakest binding to greatest release at low tissue O₂ pressure. This is a multi-step chain from raw numbers to a physiological conclusion.$px$ WHERE id = '7983142c-82d3-46a5-8e2b-fbb6f97890e9';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' For an X-linked recessive trait, a daughter is affected only when she is homozygous recessive (\(X^c X^c\)), which requires that she inherit a recessive allele from both parents; a female must receive a recessive X-linked allele from each parent in order to express the trait. Because a father contributes his single \(X\) to every daughter (and his \(Y\) only to sons), and because a male is hemizygous (needing only one recessive X allele to be affected), any father whose \(X\) carries the recessive allele is himself color blind. (Choice A) is correct: the affected daughter's \(X^c\) alleles must come one from each parent, so the father's \(X\) carries the recessive allele, and a hemizygous male with that \(X\) is himself affected (\(X^c Y\)); the unaffected mother supplies the other \(X^c\) as a carrier. (Choice B) is incorrect: a male cannot silently carry an X-linked recessive allele, because he has only one \(X\); a father who transmits a recessive-bearing \(X\) is hemizygous \(X^c Y\) and therefore expresses the trait rather than being unaffected. (Choice C) is incorrect: a father passes his \(Y\) chromosome to his sons and his \(X\) chromosome to his daughters, so the \(Y\) never reaches a daughter and cannot be the route by which she receives an allele for this X-linked gene. (Choice D) is incorrect: it combines two errors. An unaffected father cannot be the source of a recessive \(X\) (he would be \(X^C Y\)), and the \(Y\) he does pass goes to sons, not daughters, so it cannot deliver the recessive allele the affected daughter needs. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the rules of X-linked recessive transmission (daughters inherit an \(X\) from each parent and males are hemizygous) to deduce a parent's phenotype from an affected offspring.$px$ WHERE id = '799571df-804e-47c0-827b-7c2f6a4fc172';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A cell biologist exposes a population of cultured cells to a DNA-damaging agent and then measures the amount of DNA in each arrested cell using a fluorescent dye. Every arrested cell contains only a single, unreplicated complement of DNA. None of them has copied its chromosomes. At which checkpoint were these cells most likely halted?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell Cycle & Mitosis (BIO).' Interphase runs in the order G1 → S → G2, and a cell copies its chromosomes only during S phase, so a cell that still holds a single, unreplicated amount of DNA has never entered S phase. At the G1 checkpoint, the cell must be ready for DNA synthesis to proceed; at the G2 checkpoint, the cell must be fully prepared for mitosis; and the metaphase checkpoint acts before the signal is given to separate the chromosomes during anaphase. A checkpoint that acted only after replication could not produce cells whose DNA had never doubled. (Choice A) is incorrect: the G2 checkpoint operates after S phase, so any cell arrested there would already contain a doubled amount of DNA, which contradicts the single unreplicated complement the biologist measured. (Choice B) is incorrect: the metaphase (spindle) checkpoint acts even later, during mitosis, after both replication and G2, so cells stopped there would likewise have twice the DNA and their chromosomes condensed, not an unreplicated amount. (Choice C) is correct: because the arrested cells never copied their chromosomes, they were stopped before S phase, and the only checkpoint that acts at that transition (confirming the cell is ready before DNA copying begins) is the G1 checkpoint. (Choice D) is incorrect: although DNA damage can indeed be sensed and repaired, this option again places the arrest at G2, after replication; a cell halted there would already show a doubled DNA content, so the measured single complement rules it out despite its correct mention of damage repair. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the ordering of the interphase checkpoints to the measured DNA content, inferring that an unreplicated (never-doubled) genome means the cell was stopped before S phase and therefore at the G1 checkpoint.$px$ WHERE id = '79be5e12-c80d-46de-aca3-e4e847b3c9dd';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is A because a falling glucose is sensed by hypothalamic (and pancreatic) centers that drive glucagon and epinephrine, which stimulate hepatic glycogenolysis and gluconeogenesis; the response is urgent because the brain relies on glucose minute-to-minute and needs days, not minutes, to adapt to ketones.

(Choice A) is correct: it links the counter-regulatory hormones to hepatic glucose output and explains the urgency through the brain's acute glucose dependence.
(Choice B) is incorrect: insulin lowers blood glucose and would deepen the hypoglycemia; it is suppressed, not raised, in this response.
(Choice C) is incorrect: free fatty acids do not cross the blood-brain barrier, so the brain cannot switch to them acutely. That is precisely why restoring glucose is time-critical.
(Choice D) is incorrect: skeletal muscle lacks glucose-6-phosphatase and cannot release free glucose into the blood; only the liver (and kidney) can do so.

This item rewards Skill 2 (reasoning) by requiring the student to assemble the hormonal, hepatic, and neurological pieces of the hypoglycemia defense into a coherent, time-sensitive response.$px$ WHERE id = '79e23dfe-69f4-4109-ae2f-6e924b821e27';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The answer is B because only statement III names a real benefit of quaternary assembly. Quaternary structure arises when separate polypeptide chains aggregate, and these subunits are held together mainly by noncovalent interactions, not by peptide bonds. Because a symmetric homo-oligomer is built from identical subunits, a single mutation in one monomer is represented n times in an oligomer of n monomers, so one favorable change in a single gene product is reused throughout the assembly. Cooperativity, by contrast, is defined by subunits NOT binding independently: binding of the first ligand facilitates binding of the next. (Choice A) is wrong because statement I is false: subunits in a quaternary structure are held together predominantly by noncovalent interactions, not by backbone peptide bonds (peptide bonds define primary structure within a single chain). (Choice B) is correct: statement III captures the genetic economy of symmetric homo-oligomers, where one beneficial mutation in a single gene is propagated n times across the n identical subunits, while statements I and II are false. (Choice C) is wrong because it includes the false statement I; although III is correct, subunits are not joined by peptide bonds. (Choice D) is wrong because it includes the false statement II; cooperativity is precisely the opposite of independent binding. Occupancy of one site alters the affinity of the remaining sites. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of the structural hierarchy and cooperativity to evaluate which proposed consequences of quaternary assembly are actually valid.$px$ WHERE id = '7a8eacc5-0e87-452a-8eea-260ba272c79c';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$A chemist counts the electron-density regions on the central iodine of the tetrachloroiodate ion \(\text{ICl}_4^-\) and finds four iodine-chlorine bonds together with two nonbonding electron pairs on iodine. Treating this as an \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_2\) arrangement, the molecular geometry that minimizes electron-pair repulsion is most likely$px$, explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Nature of molecules and intermolecular interactions'. The answer to this question is D because six regions of electron density around a central atom adopt an octahedral electron-domain arrangement, and for an \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_2\) species the two lone pairs occupy positions \(180^\circ\) apart (trans, one above and one below the plane) to minimize the stronger repulsion between the two lone pairs, leaving the four \(\text{I}{-}\text{Cl}\) bonding pairs in a single plane. The resulting molecular geometry (the shape defined by atom positions only) is square planar with \(\text{Cl}{-}\text{I}{-}\text{Cl}\) angles of \(90^\circ\). (Choice A) Tetrahedral at \(109.5^\circ\) is the geometry for an \(\text{AX}_4\) center with no lone pairs and only four electron-density regions; \(\text{ICl}_4^-\) has six regions, so its bonding pairs cannot spread to \(109.5^\circ\). (Choice B) Seesaw arises from an \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_1\) center with five electron-density regions in a trigonal bipyramid; this both miscounts the electron domains as five rather than six and places lone pairs incorrectly, since in any bipyramidal count the single lone pair favors an equatorial site, not an axial one. (Choice C) Treating all six regions as \(\text{I}{-}\text{Cl}\) bonds ignores the two lone pairs that the iodine electron count requires, converting the \(\text{AX}_4\text{E}_2\) case into an \(\text{AX}_6\) case and yielding the wrong octahedral molecular shape. Skill 2.$px$ WHERE id = '7be9971c-50fa-4901-bd4e-0cd13f9801ed';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Hormonal Regulation and Integration of Metabolism.'

The answer is B because an all-out ~10 s sprint outstrips oxygen delivery and must draw on the immediate anaerobic systems (creatine phosphate, then anaerobic glycolysis), whereas a ~50 min steady run is submaximal enough for mitochondria to keep pace, so it runs on the oxidative burning of fatty acids and glucose.

(Choice B) is correct: brief maximal work relies on anaerobic/creatine-phosphate energy while prolonged steady work relies on oxidative metabolism of fat and glucose. The intensity-to-system pairing is right for both events.
(Choice A) is incorrect: it reverses the two systems; oxidative fat-burning is too slow to fuel a sprint, and anaerobic glycolysis cannot be sustained for a 50-minute run without fatiguing lactate accumulation.
(Choice C) is incorrect: hepatic gluconeogenesis is a slow inter-organ process, not the sprinter's immediate fuel, and creatine phosphate lasts only seconds, so it cannot power a 50-minute run.
(Choice D) is incorrect: ketone bodies become a major fuel only in prolonged fasting, not during either exercise bout in a fed athlete.

This item rewards Skill 2 (reasoning) by requiring the student to map exercise intensity and duration onto the fuel systems that can meet each demand.$px$ WHERE id = '7bff25af-305d-4270-90dc-1f34ab3f7724';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Impulse Propagation & Synapses'. The answer is B because the all-or-none property makes every action potential identical in amplitude, so the only feature of the train that can vary with stimulus strength is how often the spikes arrive. The A&P text states that "All action potentials peak at the same voltage (+30 mV), so one action potential is not bigger than another," and that "Stronger stimuli will initiate multiple action potentials more quickly, but the individual signals are not bigger" (A&P 21984-21986); Biology 2e reinforces that "once the threshold potential is reached, the neuron always completely depolarizes" (45987-45988). A downstream neuron therefore cannot recover intensity from spike size, because there is no size difference to read; intensity is encoded only as firing frequency. (Choice A) is wrong because it denies all-or-none, assuming amplitude scales with stimulus strength, but every spike peaks at the same voltage. (Choice B) is correct because rate (frequency) is the only feature of an all-or-none train that carries the intensity signal. (Choice C) is wrong because conduction velocity is set by axon diameter and myelination, not by how intense the stimulus is: "the diameter of the axon can influence the speed of conduction" and saltatory conduction along myelinated axons is faster (A&P 22061-22069). (Choice D) is wrong because, although it correctly names frequency, it smuggles in an amplitude cue that all-or-none firing rules out, since the "largest spikes" are no taller than any others. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires chaining the all-or-none property to its consequence for downstream decoding and rejecting a partially correct frequency-plus-amplitude trap.$px$ WHERE id = '7c0470e8-c047-450e-b84a-c2495d36c9e1';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Impulse Propagation & Synapses'. The answer is B because \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) entry through the presynaptic voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels is the obligatory trigger for vesicle fusion, so blocking those channels prevents neurotransmitter release even though the action potential still arrives. The text states that "When an action potential reaches the axon terminals, voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels in the membrane of the synaptic end bulb open. The concentration of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) increases inside the end bulb... The \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) facilitates the merging of the vesicle with the presynaptic membrane so that the neurotransmitter is released through exocytosis into the synaptic cleft" (A&P 2e). Likewise, Biology 2e notes that "voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels open and allow \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) to enter the cell. The calcium entry causes synaptic vesicles to fuse with the membrane and release neurotransmitter." If the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels are blocked, the depolarization arrives but no \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) enters, vesicles do not fuse, and the cleft never receives transmitter. (Choice A) is wrong because the depolarization is only an upstream step; the text makes \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) influx, not depolarization alone, the event that "facilitates the merging of the vesicle with the presynaptic membrane." (Choice B) is correct because with no \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) entry there is no fusion and therefore no release into the synaptic cleft. (Choice C) reverses the causal relationship: blocking \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) entry prevents fusion rather than locking vesicles permanently fused and releasing transmitter continuously. (Choice D) is wrong because the toxin acts only on presynaptic \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels; the postsynaptic ligand-gated receptors are a separate component and, in any case, no transmitter is released for them to bind. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because it requires predicting the downstream consequence of a specific molecular perturbation by reasoning through the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\)-dependent fusion step of the release mechanism.$px$ WHERE id = '7c432d8c-b87b-4dcc-bb53-236aea938d64';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Transmission of Heritable Information from Generation to Generation.' The answer is B because a chromosome inversion is the detachment, 180° rotation, and reinsertion of part of a chromosome, which reverses the order of the affected genes within the same chromosome; unless they disrupt a gene sequence, inversions only change gene orientation and involve no net gain or loss of genetic material. The described event (reversal of gene order on a single chromosome with no material lost or added) matches this definition exactly. (Choice A) A reciprocal translocation occurs when a chromosome segment dissociates and reattaches to a different, nonhomologous chromosome, moving the segment to another chromosome rather than flipping it in place. (Choice B) is correct: the segment is excised, rotated 180°, and reinserted into the same chromosome, reversing gene orientation with no net change in genetic content. (Choice C) A segmental deletion is when a chromosomal segment is lost, removing genetic material, but here no material is lost. (Choice D) A segmental duplication is when a chromosomal segment is copied, adding genetic material, but here no material is gained. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall the definitions of inversion, translocation, deletion, and duplication and match the described event to the correct category.$px$ WHERE id = '7d087acd-7f40-48d2-90f2-1b79f6dc352b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Impulse Propagation & Synapses.' Neurotransmitter release is not triggered directly by the depolarization itself but by a specific ion that enters the terminal when the depolarization arrives. When an action potential reaches the axon terminals, voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels in the membrane of the synaptic end bulb open, and the resulting \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) entry facilitates the merging of the vesicle with the presynaptic membrane so that neurotransmitter is released through exocytosis into the synaptic cleft. Thus the depolarizing action potential opens voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels, and the resulting rise in intracellular \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is the signal that binds vesicle proteins and drives fusion. (Choice A) is incorrect: \(\text{K}^+\) efflux through voltage-gated potassium channels repolarizes the membrane back toward rest, terminating the depolarization rather than triggering vesicle fusion. (Choice B) is correct: depolarization of the terminal opens voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels, and the influx of \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) is what directly causes the vesicles to fuse with the presynaptic membrane and release transmitter. (Choice C) is incorrect: \(\text{Na}^+\) influx through voltage-gated sodium channels propagates the action potential along the axon and depolarizes the terminal, but it is the calcium that enters afterward, not the sodium itself, that couples that depolarization to fusion. (Choice D) is incorrect: \(\text{Cl}^-\) entry is a hyperpolarizing/inhibitory event at postsynaptic membranes that moves the membrane away from threshold, and it neither opens in response to presynaptic depolarization here nor triggers release. This is a Knowledge of Scientific Concepts and Principles question because you must recall that presynaptic depolarization opens voltage-gated \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) channels and that the \(\text{Ca}^{2+}\) influx is the specific trigger for synaptic vesicle fusion.$px$ WHERE id = '7d1fe515-2d67-488c-b94c-d08216e46a2b';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The velocity-versus-pH profile of an enzyme is bell-shaped because catalysis depends on specific ionizable side chains in the active site being in the correct protonation (charge) state; for example, a side chain that must be deprotonated for optimal activity gives rise to increasing activity with pH over a particular range, and a deprotonated histidine can be required for optimal catalysis. Because different enzymes rely on different ionizable residues with different pKa values, each has its own characteristic optimal pH rather than a universal one, so a gastric protease can peak near pH 2 while another enzyme peaks near pH 8. (Choice A) is correct: the optimum reflects the pH at which the enzyme's catalytic residues carry the charges needed for substrate binding and turnover, and because those residues (and their pKa values) differ between enzymes, the two optima fall at different pH values. (Choice B) is incorrect: substrate concentration is held constant in the experiment, and even if it varied, saturation raises the plateau velocity (relates to \(V_{max}\)) without relocating the pH optimum, which is set by protonation states, not by \([S]\). (Choice C) is incorrect: pH has a marked effect on the velocity of enzyme-catalyzed reactions, and optima are enzyme-specific rather than uniformly neutral; while extreme pH can cause irreversible denaturation, that would abolish the peak, not manufacture a reproducible single maximum at pH 2 or pH 8. (Choice D) is incorrect: disulfide bonds contribute to conformational stability, but stability does not determine the pH at which the reaction rate is maximal. The position of the optimum is governed by the protonation state of catalytic residues, so a more stable enzyme would not automatically be optimal at low pH. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the principle that an enzyme's pH optimum reflects the protonation states of its ionizable catalytic side chains to explain why two enzymes with different active-site residues peak at different pH values.$px$ WHERE id = '7d6e217d-1546-4388-a079-6efc22efe02e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is B because the rate of gas diffusion across the respiratory membrane rises with the total surface area available and falls as the distance gas must travel increases; the two lesions described map cleanly onto these two separate variables. In Patient 1, destruction of alveolar walls fuses many small sacs into fewer large ones, which shrinks the aggregate internal surface area while leaving the remaining membrane thin. This is a surface-area problem. In Patient 2, the walls and sac count are preserved, but a new protein-rich layer inserted between the alveolar epithelium and capillary endothelium physically widens the barrier gas must cross. This is a diffusion-distance problem. (Choice B) is correct: it correctly assigns the loss of alveolar walls (fewer, larger sacs) to reduced surface area and the interstitial deposit (a thicker interposed barrier) to increased diffusion distance. (Choice A) is incorrect: merging alveoli into larger chambers does not thicken the membrane (the remaining walls are explicitly still thin), so Patient 1's defect is surface area, not distance; the claim conflates a bigger chamber with a longer diffusion path. (Choice C) is incorrect: it reverses both assignments. Patient 1 lost walls (surface area) rather than gaining barrier thickness, and Patient 2's deposit thickens the membrane rather than plugging air sacs to remove surface area. (Choice D) is incorrect: nothing in Patient 2 collapses alveoli or removes their walls; the sacs remain intact and numerous, so surface area is preserved and the defect is the widened barrier, meaning the two patients do not share the same mechanism. The reasoning skill exercised is applying the determinants of membrane gas diffusion to distinguish two mechanisms from structural data (AAMC Skill 2: Scientific Reasoning and Problem-Solving).$px$ WHERE id = '7da9b25e-274a-4532-9c0b-ad70af73263a';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO).' Epithelial tissue comes in subtypes whose architecture is matched to function: a single layer of very flat cells is built for movement of substances across it, whereas multiple stacked layers, tall columnar cells, and specialized glandular cells serve protection, absorption, and secretion respectively. The text states that when squamous epithelial cells are "arranged in a single layer (simple epithelia), they facilitate diffusion in tissues, such as the areas of gas exchange in the lungs and the exchange of nutrients and waste at blood capillaries." The key is to reason from the described structure (a single, extremely thin flat layer forming a barrier a fraction of a micrometer thick) to the function that geometry enables. (Choice A) is incorrect: protection against abrasion is provided by stratified (multi-layered) squamous epithelium, which the text ties to "protection... from outside abrasion and damage"; the stem explicitly describes a single thin layer, so a many-layered shedding architecture is the opposite of what is present, and thickness would impede exchange. (Choice B) is correct: a single layer of extremely flat cells minimizes the distance a molecule must travel, and because the diffusion barrier is only a fraction of a micrometer thick, dissolved gases can move rapidly between the air in the sac and the blood in the capillary. This is the exact advantage the text assigns to simple squamous epithelium at gas-exchange surfaces. (Choice C) is incorrect: mucus secretion is carried out by goblet cells and glandular epithelium; secreting a mucus film requires specialized secretory cells, which the described flat, thin, non-glandular layer lacks, and a mucus coat would add distance rather than shorten it. (Choice D) is incorrect: absorbing material from a lumen and passing it into circulation is the job of tall simple columnar epithelium in the digestive tract, not of the thin flat cells lining an air sac, and gas exchange is driven by diffusion across the barrier rather than by cellular absorption. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the structure-function relationship of epithelial subtypes, inferring from a single, extremely thin flat layer that its advantage is minimizing diffusion distance for exchange, rather than protection, secretion, or absorption.$px$ WHERE id = '7e2de1a0-d96b-4a12-a8c3-edafdfea5bd0';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is B because cellulose is a homopolysaccharide in which \(D\)-glucose residues are joined by \(\beta\text{-1,4}\) glycosidic bonds. The \(\beta\) configuration at the anomeric carbon flips every other glucose unit \(180^\circ\), producing long, straight, unbranched chains that pack side by side through extensive hydrogen bonding into rigid microfibrils, the structural basis of the plant cell wall.

(Choice A) is incorrect: \(\alpha\text{-1,4}\) linkages between glucose units are the hallmark of the storage polysaccharides starch (amylose) and glycogen, not cellulose. The \(\alpha\) linkage curls the chain into a helix suited for energy storage rather than the extended fibers cellulose forms.

(Choice C) is incorrect: \(\alpha\text{-1,6}\) linkages are the branch points found in glycogen and amylopectin, where a side chain attaches to a main \(\alpha\text{-1,4}\) chain; they do not form the linear backbone of any glucose homopolymer, and cellulose is unbranched.

(Choice B) is correct: \(\beta\text{-1,4}\)-linked glucose gives the straight, hydrogen-bonded fibers that make cellulose the main structural component of plant cell walls.

(Choice D) is incorrect: a \(\beta\text{-1,4}\)-linked polymer describes chitin, whose repeating unit is \(N\)-acetylglucosamine rather than glucose; chitin is the structural material of arthropod exoskeletons and fungal walls, not cellulose.

This question asks you to recall a single defined fact, the monomer and glycosidic linkage of cellulose, placing it at the knowledge-retrieval (Skill 1) level.$px$ WHERE id = '7ed5cdd5-3aba-4518-a3f0-0959d01ff295';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' On a Lineweaver-Burk plot, the y-intercept equals \(1/V_{max}\) and the x-intercept equals \(-1/K_M\), so a change in either intercept reports a change in that kinetic parameter. For a competitive inhibitor, \(V_{max}\) does not change but \(K_M\) appears to increase, so the family of lines shares the same y-intercept \(1/V_{max}\) while the x-intercepts \(-1/K_M\) move closer and closer to 0 as inhibitor concentration rises. These intersecting plots are the hallmark of competitive inhibition. (Choice A) is correct: an unchanged y-intercept means \(V_{max}\) is preserved, while steeper slopes and x-intercepts approaching the origin mean apparent \(K_M\) rises. That is the exact signature of a competitive inhibitor, which binds the free enzyme and is outcompeted at high substrate. (Choice B) is incorrect: uncompetitive inhibition lowers both \(V_{max}\) and \(K_M\) proportionally, producing parallel lines with the same slope but a raised y-intercept, not lines converging on a shared y-intercept. (Choice C) is incorrect: pure noncompetitive inhibition lowers \(V_{max}\) while leaving \(K_M\) unchanged, so the lines share the same x-intercept and diverge at a higher y-intercept, the mirror image of the described pattern. (Choice D) is incorrect: irreversible inhibition permanently inactivates enzyme rather than reversibly competing for binding, so it cannot be overcome by raising substrate and is not classified by these reversible Lineweaver-Burk line families. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the relationship between Lineweaver-Burk intercepts and \(V_{max}\)/\(K_M\) to translate a described change in plot geometry into the underlying inhibition mechanism.$px$ WHERE id = '7f0d76f1-4581-4237-b71c-5ffca71dcb35';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$On a hot day, a person's facial skin flushes as arterioles feeding the dermal capillary beds widen, and separately their skin becomes slick as glandular secretions evaporate from its surface. In the respiratory tract, which single response uses the same heat-transfer mechanism as the skin flushing, and not the mechanism used by the slick skin?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is B because the described skin flushing is cutaneous vasodilation. Widening of arterioles increases blood flow through surface capillaries so that warm blood radiates (conducts) heat directly across the tissue. The airway response using this identical mechanism is vasodilation of the nasal and tracheal mucosal capillary beds, which delivers warm blood to the airway surface to dump heat. The 'slick skin' response is a different mechanism (evaporative cooling of secreted water), so the correct airway parallel must be the vascular/radiative one, not an evaporative one. (Choice B) is correct: dilating the mucosal capillary beds is the airway equivalent of cutaneous vasodilation. Both move warm blood to a surface so heat is lost by radiation/conduction rather than by evaporation. (Choice A) is incorrect: evaporation of airway water is the respiratory parallel of the slick, evaporating skin (sweat), not of the flushing/vasodilation the question asks to match. (Choice C) is incorrect: panting increases evaporative heat loss by driving more airflow over the wet mucosa, so it mirrors the evaporative (slick-skin) mechanism, again the one the stem excludes. (Choice D) is incorrect: vasoconstriction reduces surface blood flow to conserve heat. It is the opposite (cold-response) of vasodilation, so it neither matches the flushing nor cools the body. The skill required is integrating parallel thermoregulatory mechanisms across two organ systems and discriminating radiative from evaporative heat loss.$px$ WHERE id = '7fb863fa-e5e9-4995-81c4-b95d7a016624';
UPDATE public.questions SET question_text = $px$During a normal resting breath, a subject inhales and then allows the breath to leave without any conscious effort. The chest and abdomen simply settle back as air flows out. Which event drives this stage of the resting breathing cycle?$px$, explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Organ Systems.' The answer is C because quiet exhalation at rest is passive: the inspiratory muscles (diaphragm and external intercostals) simply relax, and the elastic tissue of the lung and thoracic wall, stretched during inhalation, recoils to its resting size, raising intrapulmonary pressure above atmospheric pressure and pushing air out with no muscular contraction required. (Choice C) is correct: with the inspiratory muscles relaxed, elastic recoil of the lung and chest wall shrinks the thoracic cavity and expels air, which is why resting exhalation costs essentially no muscular effort. (Choice A) is incorrect: contraction of the internal intercostals and abdominal muscles produces active, forced exhalation (as in exercise or coughing), not the effortless exhalation of a resting breath. (Choice B) is incorrect: contraction and flattening of the diaphragm enlarges the thoracic cavity to drive inhalation, the opposite phase of the cycle. (Choice D) is incorrect: contraction of the external intercostals lifts the rib cage to expand the chest during inhalation, not exhalation. Skill: this item tests the ability to identify the physiological mechanism underlying a described phase of the respiratory cycle.$px$ WHERE id = '8087ec77-27c5-45e6-be20-1021acf58502';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biology question that falls under the content category 'Cell and Tissue Structure (BIO)'. The answer is A because elastin is the fiber that lets a stretched wall return to its original shape, so losing it specifically abolishes recoil while leaving the wall otherwise structurally laid down. The text states that "The main property of elastin is that after being stretched or compressed, it will return to its original shape," and that "Dense irregular elastic tissues give arterial walls the strength and the ability to regain original shape after stretching." In a large elastic artery, this recoil is what stores the energy of systolic stretch and releases it between beats to keep blood moving forward; remove functional elastin and the wall still stretches during systole but cannot spring back, so it loses recoil while collagen and ground substance keep their normal roles. (Choice A) Correct: with elastin gone, the wall stretches but does not return to its original diameter, which is exactly the loss of recoil the text attributes to elastin. (Choice B) Wrong: resisting stretch and providing tensile strength are properties of collagen fibers, which "have great tensile strength, resist stretching"; because collagen is laid down normally here, the wall still resists stretch, so rupture is not the direct consequence of the elastin defect. This is a collagen-versus-elastin swap. (Choice C) Wrong: trapping moisture is a property of the proteoglycan ground substance, which "attracts and traps available moisture," not of elastin; ground substance is deposited normally in this patient, so water retention is unaffected. (Choice D) Wrong: the vessel lumen is lined by endothelium, a type of epithelium, but elastin is a connective-tissue fiber that confers recoil, not the basal-lamina anchor that attaches epithelial cells. This conflates the connective-tissue defect with an unrelated epithelial readback. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the specific structure-function role of elastin to predict the physiological outcome of its loss in a particular vessel and rule out properties that belong to other matrix components.$px$ WHERE id = '80f7790a-5d89-465d-90a5-3d5caab72d5e';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Metabolism of Fatty Acids and Proteins.'

The answer is C because the protective process must be the one that removes cholesterol from the site where it would otherwise accumulate. Process 2 carries excess cholesterol out of peripheral tissues and back to the liver, lowering the cholesterol available to deposit in artery walls. Process 1 moves cholesterol in the opposite direction, toward the periphery, which supplies the very tissues where deposits form. The reasoning is pure transport logic from the supplied directions, not recall of particle names.

(Choice C) is correct: Process 2 moves cholesterol away from peripheral tissues toward the liver, reducing what remains to accumulate in artery walls, so it is protective.
(Choice A) is incorrect: delivering more cholesterol to peripheral tissues (Process 1) increases the local supply that can deposit in artery walls; adding cholesterol to a site does not protect it from buildup.
(Choice B) is incorrect: it names the correct process but reverses its logic; returning cholesterol to the liver removes it from artery walls rather than increasing delivery into them.
(Choice D) is incorrect: direction clearly matters. Moving cholesterol toward versus away from a tissue changes whether it accumulates there, which is the whole basis of the comparison.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, it tests transport logic (which direction of movement clears versus deposits cholesterol) rather than class-name recall.$px$ WHERE id = '80fbc6b8-a4b4-4e08-a72e-c6dd20b35ae6';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Carbohydrates.'

The answer is D because the only structural difference described is the group at C-2: a hydroxyl (\(-\text{OH}\)) versus a hydrogen (\(-\text{H}\)). In a polynucleotide, that C-2 hydroxyl sits adjacent to the phosphodiester bond of the backbone and can act as an intramolecular nucleophile: under basic conditions it attacks the neighboring phosphorus, cleaving the chain. This is precisely why ribose-containing polymers (RNA) are chemically labile and self-cleave, whereas replacing the C-2 \(-\text{OH}\) with \(-\text{H}\) (the deoxy modification) removes that nucleophile and yields a far more stable backbone (DNA). The consequence is read directly from the single structural change, not from the name of the sugar.

(Choice D) is correct: The reactive C-2 hydroxyl provides an intramolecular nucleophile positioned to attack the backbone phosphate, so its presence increases susceptibility to base-catalyzed self-cleavage, the correct consequence.

(Choice B) is incorrect: This reverses the actual relationship: the C-2 hydroxyl does not shield the phosphate; it makes the backbone more, not less, vulnerable to nucleophilic (self-)cleavage. The sugar lacking that hydroxyl is the more stable one.

(Choice C) is incorrect: Ring closure (hemiacetal formation) depends on the anomeric carbon (C-1) reacting with a distal ring-oxygen donor, not on the C-2 substituent; both the hydroxyl- and hydrogen-bearing sugars cyclize normally, so the C-2 group does not prevent ring formation.

(Choice A) is incorrect: 'Reducing sugar' status depends on a free anomeric carbon (the aldehyde/hemiacetal at C-1), which both sugars possess; a substituent at C-2 does not determine whether the sugar reduces Benedict's reagent, so this consequence does not follow from the C-2 hydroxyl.

As a Skill 2 (reasoning about scientific concepts) item, this question asks you to reason from a single defined structural feature, a hydroxyl versus a hydrogen at C-2, to its chemical consequence for backbone reactivity, rather than to recall a definition.$px$ WHERE id = '81c33cac-b7a5-4355-9cdc-e43086441f56';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a General Chemistry question that falls under the content category 'Acid-Base Equilibria (GC, BC)'. The answer to this question is C because buffer capacity (the amount of added acid or base a buffer can absorb before its \(\text{pH}\) shifts substantially) increases with the total concentration of the conjugate acid-base pair. Both buffers begin at the same \(\text{pH}\) of \(4.74\) because the Henderson-Hasselbalch relation \(\text{pH} = \text{p}K_a + \log([\text{A}^-]/[\text{HA}])\) depends only on the \(\text{p}K_a\) and the ratio, both of which are identical here. When the same small number of moles of strong acid is added, it converts the same number of moles of \(\text{CH}_3\text{COO}^-\) to \(\text{CH}_3\text{COOH}\) in each beaker. In Buffer X, that fixed change is a much smaller fraction of the large \(0.50\,\text{M}\) reserves, so the \([\text{A}^-]/[\text{HA}]\) ratio barely moves and the \(\text{pH}\) changes very little. In Buffer Y, the identical change is a much larger fraction of the small \(0.050\,\text{M}\) amounts, so the ratio, and hence the \(\text{pH}\), shifts more. The concentrated Buffer X therefore has the greater buffer capacity and undergoes the smaller \(\text{pH}\) change. (Choice A) is wrong because it confuses the factors that set the starting \(\text{pH}\) (the \(\text{p}K_a\) and the conjugate ratio) with the factor that sets buffer capacity (the absolute concentrations); two buffers can share an identical \(\text{pH}\) yet have very different capacities. (Choice B) is wrong because it reverses the actual relationship: a more dilute buffer holds fewer moles of conjugate base to consume the incoming \(\text{H}^+\), so it is overwhelmed sooner and its \(\text{pH}\) shifts more, not less; "more reactive" is not a meaningful basis for capacity. (Choice D) is wrong because the \(\text{p}K_a\) values are stated to be identical (both are acetate buffers), so \(\text{p}K_a\) cannot be the discriminating factor; capacity here is governed by concentration alone, not by any difference in \(\text{p}K_a\). Skill 2$px$ WHERE id = '81f986b6-315f-4044-a20e-69fb8f563b68';
UPDATE public.questions SET explanation = $px$This is a Biochemistry question that falls under the content category 'Structure and Function of Proteins and Their Constituent Amino Acids.' The six EC classes are defined by the type of reaction catalyzed, and the first digit of an enzyme's EC number encodes that class. The reaction described is glycolysis step 2, catalyzed by phosphoglucose isomerase, which rearranges a single substrate into a single product of identical formula. To classify it you cannot match a keyword in the stem to a class name; you must compare the substrate and product and ask what actually changed. (Choice A) is correct: glucose-6-phosphate and fructose-6-phosphate have the same molecular formula, and the enzyme merely shifts the carbonyl from C1 (an aldose) to C2 (a ketose) within one molecule, with no group gained, lost, or exchanged with a second molecule. Rearranging one molecule into an isomer of itself is the diagnostic reaction of an isomerase (EC 5). (Choice B) is incorrect: transferases (EC 2) move an intact functional group from a donor molecule to an acceptor molecule, so a student who fixates on the word 'phosphate' in the substrate may assume a group is being handed off, but the stem states the phosphate ester is untouched and no second substrate is present, so nothing is transferred between molecules. (Choice C) is incorrect: oxidoreductases (EC 1) catalyze electron transfer, and converting an aldehyde (aldose) to a ketone (ketose) superficially resembles the aldehyde↔ketone chemistry of redox reactions; however, this interconversion proceeds through an enediol intermediate with no external electron acceptor and no cofactor such as NAD⁺, so the overall oxidation state of the sugar is unchanged. (Choice D) is incorrect: lyases (EC 4) break a C-C, C-O, or C-N bond by a non-hydrolytic, non-oxidative route, usually splitting one substrate into two products or creating a new double bond; here a single product of the same formula is formed and the carbon skeleton stays intact, so no bond is cleaved to yield a second fragment. This is a Scientific Reasoning and Problem Solving question because you must apply the definitions of the EC classes to a described reaction and infer, from the fact that one molecule is rearranged into an isomer of itself, that the enzyme is an isomerase rather than a transferase, oxidoreductase, or lyase.$px$ WHERE id = '8233e99d-b761-4a4f-a396-b1a512bcd631';

COMMIT;
